/**
 * English translations of the interview quotes (src/data/CMS/citas.js), keyed by id_cita.
 * The Spanish original stays the source of truth; the site shows these through the
 * "Show English translation" toggle. Kept apart from the CMS export so a re-export
 * of the spreadsheet does not overwrite them.
 */
const citasEn = {
  "CIT001": "Because, incredibly, we always say this, this species taught us to be better human beings. The time we shared with them, day and night, and I say shared because we no longer saw them as a species outside of us; rather, we saw the species becoming a kind of family to us too.",
  "CIT002": "Thanks to the phenology that has been carried out for several years, we now have marked the dates when some of the species we need for the nursery reproduce. For many there are already records, but not for all. So, based on this, we already have the phenology data, when there is flowering, when there is fruit, and the collections go out. Sometimes some hit in the dry season, some hit in the rainy season, so you always have to stay on top of that. And besides that, you learn to recognize the fruits and the seeds, and to recognize the seedlings. That is a really beautiful and interesting subject that is sometimes a bit hard, but you do manage it.",
  "CIT003": "It had already been established as a migration time for these species because they lived in the ranch for a while, but then they left, and at that moment nobody knew where they went. But knowing that the densest forest there was lay between the forest reserve and Corcovado National Park, we understood that they migrated to Corcovado and then returned to the forest reserve, which at that time was not yet a reserve, but there was a whole belt of forest around the park and they migrated here. The hunters were starting to learn that information, and so when those dates approached, they came to the town to hunt. That is how many, many populations disappeared, until their numbers were minimal, and some were very hard to find here.",
  "CIT004": "This group is dedicated to, as I was saying, setting up monitoring routes, walking routes you might say, because sometimes we don't fully understand the word monitoring; but well, three routes are set up in the community, and once a month these areas are visited, these routes, almost always by the same people at the same times, to observe species, whether birds, or the phenology of plants, or tracking mammals, observing them visually or also with camera traps.",
  "CIT005": "But other organizations came back and showed us other paths, opening other doors like joining efforts, working as a team, as UCR did, as UNA has, as even, up to a point, SINAC did, and some other institutions like IMAS and INA. They have trained and empowered very many sectors of the population, so that it was no longer each person working individually but working as a community team, and that is also how a group was born, the group that today in the community is in charge of protecting the environment, the community biological monitoring group, of which I am fortunately a part.",
  "CIT006": "Because, say, for example, we did a patrol yesterday. Today we came in the morning to pick up the tracks of these herds again. So we could tell what time they had passed, by looking at some plants they ate, some seeds they bit, some roots. Sometimes in the dry season it is very hard because the ground is dry, or when the herd turns in circles, you can't tell where they went.",
  "CIT007": "You had to hunt to survive, you had to fell the forests; it was part of the culture of the community, of the country, and at the national level too, the community was involved for a couple of decades in those practices. Until the opportunities came, until a road opened, until there was access to the community both to enter and to leave, and little by little those changes began to arise. And with a road for people to come in, government institutions also came in and began to bring a spark of hope to the town, though in the early times, in the first years after these organizations arrived, they were not so well accepted.",
  "CIT008": "And maybe it would not have been so difficult if they had not been invaded by other communities, because unfortunately Rancho Quemado has suffered, or suffered, we say suffered because it no longer happens, a lot of hunting, but the hunting that was going on was not exactly by the residents of Rancho Quemado themselves but by people who came from other towns like San Vito, Piedras Blancas, Pérez Zeledón; people came to hunt from many other places in the country.",
  "CIT009": "We took turns; at times women went in accompanied by a man, or otherwise only women, to do the monitoring. At such-and-such an hour we knew we had to come out, others went in, and so on in succession, day and night. We always had a clearly marked schedule so we knew that so-and-so and so-and-so were going in. Sometimes there were groups of children with us too, monitoring in the forest or here in the community.",
  "CIT010": "They are between the boundaries of the Reserve and Corcovado. They have stayed there for more than a year. They come down, but they go back. Right now we have some of them marked with ear tags, because there was a research project where we captured them to fit radio collars, and so we took the chance to put ear tags on them to identify them within the herd. So we have camera traps in some areas where they pass, and right there we have the individuals with ear tags.",
  "CIT011": "Today we came in the morning to pick up the tracks of these herds again. So we could tell what time they had passed, by looking at some plants they ate, some seeds they bit, some roots. Sometimes in the dry season it is very hard because the ground is dry, or when the herd turns in circles, you can't tell where it went. But okay, those are hunters' strategies, and those hunters' details are what have made the monitoring, or the protection of this species, extremely rich.",
  "CIT012": "\"There we keep doing different kinds of work: environmental education for children, for young people and adults, educational talks with the elders who can pass on to us all that knowledge and those experiences that are invaluable. And today, not only in the Rancho Quemado community but we can travel to other places too, to carry out all these environmental activities that we have managed to do.\"",
  "CIT013": "When we began to understand and comprehend all these needs, well, we said we are going to do something, because we already had vast experience, more than 20 years of seeing them slaughtered, since we were children. Seeing, for example, here it was so common to see people passing with their horses and four pigs, six pigs hanging from their mounts, from their horses, right? And for us, for me as a girl, at that moment it was very shocking. And then we grew up and said, well, but what is going on, where is the organization whose job it is to protect this? And that protection of the species was not happening.",
  "CIT014": "In 2019 we met, around May, June, with other NGOs of the town, of the area, sorry, and we decided to say, we are going to put this on the table and we are going to do whatever we have to do, because we are not going to allow them to be killed anymore, the way they were doing it. It was something terrible in reality, terrible to live through that experience. So we started to monitor the forests, we did trips, maybe, I don't know, 8 or 10 hours in the forest, at the time we knew they migrated, to see whether they were starting to approach the community. And on the very first trip we immediately found the first group of peccaries, quite close to the community, and from that moment, which was on the 10th of August 2019, we will never forget it, it was like the mark they left on us. And from that moment we started monitoring every day, until we could give them the security, the protection they needed.",
  "CIT015": "Unfortunately, during the time that we have been providing them protection, yes, even while being there, for some moments, some hours when we had to leave gaps and go off to monitor, the hunters did have us monitored. So in those moments they have come in. On one occasion they wounded some of the females with machetes. On one occasion they also hunted one with bullets, right, with firearms. And my son, who was out monitoring at that moment, when he went in, found the herd completely disoriented, peccaries running everywhere, and he could not figure out how to locate the herd because there were tracks all over the place. And to his surprise he found the remains of a peccary that we thought, he did not know anything at the time, he did not know how a jaguar or a hunter could leave signs of a kill, or a jaguar, right, how to tell whether it had been a jaguar or a hunter.",
  "CIT016": "Yes, they eat birds. So it turns out that if they find a chicken farm or a hen house that is accessible, they will go in and eat them. So we lived that experience, and that is why we also had to provide protection 24/7 when they came close to the town, because they created dangers for small children, for older adults, since they are wild animals and we don't know how they are going to react.",
  "CIT017": "Yes, in the early days of the founding of the community, which was the part I told you about, when I was not there, we do have the story of the first inhabitants of Rancho Quemado, and when they settled here they tell us: we didn't have to move, the animals came right into the yard of your house.",
  "CIT018": "It had already been established as a migration time for these species because they lived in the ranch for a while but then they left, and at that moment nobody knew where they went. But knowing that the densest forest there was lay between the Forest Reserve and the Gulf, sorry, Corcovado National Park, we understand that they migrated to Corcovado and then returned to the Forest Reserve, which at that time was not yet a reserve, but there was a whole belt of forest around the park and they migrated here.",
  "CIT019": "Because, incredibly, we always say this, this species taught us to be better human beings. The time we shared with them, day and night, and I say shared because we no longer saw them as a species outside of us; rather, we saw the species becoming a kind of family to us too.",
  "CIT020": "So this group is dedicated to that, to doing that monitoring work in these three areas. Those were the initial practices in the first years of activity, but as time went by the group grew in number; more people joined, they fell in love with the activity. And we also took on more tasks, more work within the activities we do, such as environmental education. We began to see that with adults it is not so easy to bring about change, but with children there is a very high chance of change. So since 2017 we began working with environmental education, up to the present, and we now have kids who are young people now and work with us on the monitoring side.",
  "CIT021": "I took the seeds. The seeds grew and put out leaves and flowers, and for me that was wow! And then a fruit came out. For me that was something that I feel marked me deeply, because I didn't understand how something so big could come from a seed. That was for me. And later, when I went to high school, I lasted half a year because it was not for me. I ran away. I ran into my mom. My mom already knew I wasn't good at that. So I ended up at a workshop just to sweep. A cabinetmaking workshop. And so, once again, I connected with plants, without knowing the aromas of the woods. How wonderful!",
  "CIT022": "I didn't have a high school diploma. So it took me three years to get to the National Parks Service, thanks to the help of Álvaro Ugalde, who is no longer here with us. I was director of the national parks of Costa Rica, the founder of the parks together with Mario Boza. And in '85, with the invasion of Corcovado, I wrote him a letter. Why? I don't know. I wrote a letter to Álvaro Ugalde and a letter to President Monge. And I got angry with Monge because he never answered me. He never even received it. Álvaro Ugalde did receive it and called me. So I went to the office and told him it was incredible to me that I was meeting with him. And I said to him, what should a park ranger in a national park do if he's there for 20 days? And I told him, if a park ranger is there for 20 days, how is it possible that foreigners come to tell us what we have, and there are people who are there for 22 days? And that is where I said, I want to be there to see what the monkeys eat, what they are doing. And he says to me, \"Reinaldo, but we have to hire you right now.\" And he called the accountant: \"Do we have money?\" \"There's no money.\" \"Well, Reinaldo, if you want to come in as a volunteer, we'll send you to Cahuita.\"",
  "CIT023": "From the forest to the computer. I have a video that we made. But I can tell you here too. What you're asking me. Look, I have this here, right? I already have it numbered. I enter it into the database, right? It's a consecutive number. After I do that, I'm going to take the folder of the images.",
  "CIT024": "With parks, it's about getting people to fall in love, with the walks, with the observations. So the trails have to be designed to be flat. They used to tell me, it's like going to a mall. When you walk into a mall, you're already learning. The floors are clean, everything reflects, the windows there. It's exactly the same. But of course, you arrive at one of those thickets, and mosquitoes, and mud. Who is going to like that?",
  "CIT025": "So today, say, I see the UNED students when they take a short course on plants, making a sample, first thing, they aren't even taught how to do it, right? They do it really badly. The other thing is that it isn't necessary, because there's the digital part, or it has to be a combination of the two. So when you make a collection, it's at the moment you do it that you are already learning. It's not the same to come here and look at it as to hold it, you understand? To look at it, to touch it, and you already feel that, so it is extremely valuable for learning. And you also have to record it, because it's not only that you made the image, you have to record it, you have to write, and that demands more time and a bit of rigor.",
  "CIT026": "I think we all have something inside us that often doesn't come out or is repressed for many reasons, the people around us, the jobs we have, but there is always something that exists in ourselves that at some point is going to go off, it may be early or very late, but for me it's never late, and it's never early. But I think some of us have more of the, how do you say it, stubbornness, \"pigheaded,\" a friend of mine says to me, \"dude, more stubborn, more pigheaded,\" right? And I don't know where that comes from, but something I did understand, since I left school so early, is that it's like when you leave an animal free, that is, it doesn't know what those damn fences are, it jumps over them, or something like that, it cuts them. But when you... how do you say it? When you indoctrinate it, it's not something they're giving you as a cradle, but something that is social pressure.",
  "CIT027": "People are not prepared. So maybe the naturalists are the only ones who stay there. But to me, all of us have something of a naturalist in us. It's just that you have to, what do you call it? Awaken it and motivate it. So, how is it? We make a trail for people who are just starting. Flat and pretty. And you tell them, look, put on boots. Go well covered. Long sleeves, use repellent. So that you enjoy the walk.",
  "CIT028": "\"I collected plants, and do you know what it's called? No, because that is learning over time. I pick it because it has flowers and fruit; and then I start to study in that process, so it is simultaneous, collecting, learning, and of course one as a human being gets excited, what could that be? What's it called? And when you don't know, you simply leave it there. Until months later you find out what it was, and you start to make the collection. It's called, when you have something that is rare, make many specimens to send to the specialists; when it's something common you make three, because one for UCR, one for the museum and another for exchange abroad. And I picked the guayabón, I picked the terminal guayabón, and Oblonga, I made 20 collections, 20 duplicates, what's it called? I'll show you one. And of course when I got to San José Aldo says to me, 'Why did you make 20 of that if it's such a common tree?' And for me it was the first time, for me it was rare, right? So I tell that as a joke, but it's part of the learning; I mean, how can one know what is rare? And that is very, very complicated to see, but so I started on that learning curve of learning about plants.\"",
  "CIT029": "So, that taught me that... And I'll put it this way, say, to understand it. If right now I hand you a store. It could be a hardware store or a supermarket or a corner shop. You have to know what you have. You have to know what you have and how many you have and how much it's worth. And what it's for. Because if I arrive there, what do you have there? How much is it worth and what is it not good for? So, a forest works the same way. But then, to put it in reality. Let's go to the forest, to the timber. That tree they call María. But what is María? It's a semi-hard wood. A precious wood. And where is the rest of the things? So, we know nothing about the forest. That is being illiterate, really. So you speak like a local guide. The local guide knows the names. He has more... he handles more knowledge than a forester. He knows that's a wild fruit tree. He doesn't know it's a Myristicaceae or a Virola. But he knows them. And he knows it's a semi-hard wood. Why? Because he has already used them. He has burned them. He has built with them. So, I think that, as a kind of synthesis of bio-literacy, it is knowing what is inside. What is it called? That bird, that insect and that plant. It seems very ambitious. But that is how we have to be.",
  "CIT030": "I have a friend at the cooperative who says that the blue zone of the country is marked in red because it is very poor. Right? Very poor. If you're a little girl at school and you're there, how do you feel? And on one side it says they are very rich. And to me that is a fatal mistake. That should not be on a map saying, the richest places, Osa. Which don't have a building, a house made of straw. That is not wealth, you understand? So that's where the confusion starts, where people want to have more, more and more. So having a modest house, having agriculture, not anymore, because now you need a house that needs I don't know what, air conditioning, a bunch of stuff, and wealth is lost. So that message that is given everywhere here, so to me poor is someone who is under a bridge. And to generate this, well, to generate food, a roof, coins, money. And you should see, with conservation, non-profit. If you look at the organizations that exist here, non-profit, they are such big companies, and where are their offices? Osa Conservation has its offices in Washington, D.C.",
  "CIT031": "The difficulties for me are, above all, administrative. We started with a project that was what I'm saying. Just do, do and do. We had a petty cash box of 50,000 colones in 1989. What for? A soda was bought. A pump was bought. A tire for the bicycle was bought. A bicycle was bought. A gallon of gasoline was bought. Toilet paper was bought. A battery was bought. It doesn't matter. You keep going. When administration begins, \"Reinaldo, you have to get invoices for the bookstore.\" Invoices for the supermarket. Invoices for the workshop. Invoices for fuel. So then it goes, control, and the whole thing starts to veer off. What? Why did you spend that money? So the petty cash went from 50 to 25 and then disappeared. Because the philosophy of the project was lost. The idea is that you walk. And we were trained to walk, to do. It didn't matter what happened, but then comes the administration.",
  "CIT032": "What I see is, say, the natural areas. You have the Ministry of Health, which employs people, right? The natural areas also employ those people. But the most curious thing is that in all projects people from all careers are required. Except biologists. And that I don't understand. When we talk about anything, nothing is said about biology. And biology is inside all of us. In all of us, all day long. But that is never talked about. So it is work that we have right there, but nobody pays attention to it. So, for example, mental or physical health, or food, or medicine, are here in these areas. But there is no effort to understand that. So you have MINAE, which gave me access. And on the other side, the institutions that are very focused on all of that. But on one side there is no contribution toward, say, ICE works with the water sources. And who produces them? The protected areas. Tourism too. But you are not a source that feeds that.",
  "CIT033": "So one way is with evidence. Some are tracks, right? Tracks by means of bones, droppings or footprints. Or visual observations. So then you know what it has. So, say, if in that area, I don't know, 15 hectares of forest, and there are no animals, it means it is a destroyed place, you see? But if there is presence, and then over time, you can tell, ah, look, after 10 years, see how successful we are. From having a few footprints to the number of footprints now. And the number of observations. That's why. It is a way of having what I call having the pulse of what is happening. And knowing, so, and this is where the word that everyone uses comes in. Global warming. Ugh, what a drag. Of course. But how do we really know the effects? How do we measure them? We have data from the past. We have monitoring from the past to know that. So everybody talks about that.",
  "CIT034": "When you pick up plants, it's like opening a tap of knowledge and starting to see other things. So, for me, plants teach me to see the insects, and the birds, and the snakes. They are not disconnected, you see? So, when you can go deeper into one group, into a group, you have the chance to broaden your knowledge without really studying those groups. Do you follow me? Do you know what I want to tell you? So you may be studying snakes, amphibians, but if you like it, you can understand more about the context, which plants are there. So, one learns.",
  "CIT035": "You see, taxonomists are generally very shy; they are not people who go around giving classes or anything, because their life is simply to put us on the plants. And we, as a group with Daniel Janzen in Santa Rosa, had the opportunity that a taxonomist would give us a class. Because we were a small group, so for them it was like feeling young, because they were all old and we were just kids. So they would tell us something and we would go into that mountain and bring them things, and they motivated us and gave us the energy and the interest to learn. So that knowledge was, let's say, wonderful for us.",
  "CIT036": "So, that taught me that... And I'll put it this way, say, to understand it. If right now I hand you a store. It could be a hardware store or a supermarket or a corner shop. You have to know what you have. You have to know what you have and how many you have and how much it's worth. And what it's for. Because if I arrive there, what do you have there? How much is it worth and what is it not good for? So, a forest works the same way. But then, to put it in reality. Let's go to the forest, to the timber. That tree they call María. But what is María? It's a semi-hard wood. A precious wood. And where is the rest of the things? So, we know nothing about the forest. That is being illiterate, really. So you speak like a local guide. The local guide knows the names. He has more... he handles more knowledge than a forester. He knows that's a wild fruit tree. He doesn't know it's a Myristicaceae or a Virola. But he knows them. And he knows it's a semi-hard wood. Why? Because he has already used them. He has burned them. He has built with them. So, I think that, as a kind of synthesis of bio-literacy, it is knowing what is inside. What is it called? That bird, that insect and that plant. It seems very ambitious. But that is how we have to be.",
  "CIT037": "And there, Daniel says, \"you have to write.\" So we... right? Because we had never touched a keyboard. And Daniel said, \"you have to learn.\" So it became clear to me that at that moment we had to use computers. That was a reality. And fortunately, I was very stubborn, and when INBio took me on, I wanted... My notebook, which had 5 thousand entries, I typed it in about three times. Because there is something the computer people call optimizing. I never knew what that was. I know that there can't be a stray tab, a space, and now I've learned it, but it cost me a great deal of time, right?",
  "CIT038": "I collected plants, and do you know what it's called? No, because that is learning over time. I pick it because it has flowers and fruit; and then I start to study in that process, so it is simultaneous, collecting, learning, and of course one as a human being gets excited, what could that be? What's it called? And when you don't know, you simply leave it there. Until months later you find out what it was, and you start to make the collection. It's called, when you have something that is rare, make many specimens to send to the specialists; when it's something common you make three, because one for UCR, one for the museum and another for exchange abroad. And I picked the guayabón, I picked the terminal guayabón, and Oblonga, I made 20 collections, 20 duplicates, what's it called? I'll show you one. And of course when I got to San José Aldo says to me, \"Why did you make 20 of that if it's such a common tree?\" And for me it was the first time, for me it was rare, right? So I tell that as a joke, but it's part of the learning; I mean, how can one know what is rare? And that is very, very complicated to see, but so I started on that learning curve of learning about plants.",
  "CIT039": "Plants. But, as a summary, when I was in school, someone asks me, why did you study plants? I say, I don't know. A coincidence of life. When I was in school, I never forget the cucumber seeds they gave me for homework. It was 20 minutes a week of agriculture where I was a true genius. The rest of the classes I felt like the stupidest person. Really ugly, honestly. I don't want to remember that part. Only the half hour of agriculture.",
  "CIT040": "I had already arrived in Osa with a botanist who brought me; I had about 3 months of work because we were doing a job with insects with the parataxonomist program with Daniel Janzen in 1989 in Santa Rosa. And in Braulio Carrillo the budget ran out, because there is no data, research is not important, it is not necessary, and so 3 people had to be cut. The 3 of us were the parataxonomists of the research program, because we didn't do anything, we wanted... if we set out the light for the insects, we collected insects, collected plants, generated evolution, cooked, et cetera, et cetera, but that was not work.",
  "CIT041": "I collected plants, and do you know what it's called? No, because that is learning over time. I pick it because it has flowers and fruit; and then I start to study in that process, so it is simultaneous, collecting, learning, and of course one as a human being gets excited, what could that be? What's it called? And when you don't know, you simply leave it there. Until months later you find out what it was, and you start to make the collection. It's called, when you have something that is rare, make many specimens to send to the specialists; when it's something common you make three, because one for UCR, one for the museum and another for exchange abroad. And I picked the guayabón, I picked the terminal guayabón, and Oblonga, I made 20 collections, 20 duplicates, what's it called? I'll show you one. And of course when I got to San José Aldo says to me, \"Why did you make 20 of that if it's such a common tree?\" And for me it was the first time, for me it was rare, right? So I tell that as a joke, but it's part of the learning; I mean, how can one know what is rare?",
  "CIT042": "So I get to San José. Haven't you seen my car, you haven't seen the inside. \"Reinaldo, that car is dirty. Where does it come from?\" \"From the Osa Peninsula.\" \"Well, there's no asphalt on the roads there.\" So I keep that car like that because they told me so many times that the car is a tool. You use it. It rolled over, it crashed. It's a tool. Because we see it as something that, ah, no, it has to be spotless. I don't do that. So my car is full. So he calls me, \"Reinaldo,\" again, \"Because you came in with boots full of mud.\" And I was going to tell him, \"look what's in those baskets. Full of red things there. Don't tell me the mud is dirty, please.\" So, no, no. And that was what cost me the most. Always. Because I'm too much of an animal. Too much of the countryside. And administrative things I often can't tolerate. No, well, I can't. So I was the rebel. When \"Reinaldo,\" again, he said, my God, me again. So, \"why not, no, no?\" So a friend tells me one day, \"dude, damn, don't speak, stay quiet.\"",
  "CIT043": "And so that is going to have two effects. And for me, it's somewhat right, but it doesn't apply. Once, if you didn't do it, find something to do. But for me, parks are not that. With parks, it's about getting people to fall in love, with the walks, with the observations. So the trails have to be designed to be flat. They used to tell me, it's like going to a mall. When you walk into a mall, you're already learning. The floors are clean, everything reflects, the windows there. It's exactly the same. But of course, you arrive at one of those thickets, and mosquitoes, and mud. Who is going to like that? People are not prepared. So maybe the naturalists are the only ones who stay there. But to me, all of us have something of a naturalist in us. It's just that you have to, what do you call it? Awaken it and motivate it.",
  "CIT044": "And if you look at what they eat now and what we used to eat, that's it. There is nothing. Right? All of it purely, if you want to buy cassava here, you have to go to the supermarket and it already comes with wax, because people don't grow it here. Corn. So a friend of mine told me, Reinaldo, look, us, look at those hens, that corn, now I'm buying it. But the corn I used to feed the hens, I produced it. And the rice I ate, I produced it. But since they're going to throw us out of here, they say, the gringos are going to throw us out. I said, dude, where did that come from? But there is a very widespread idea. This part here, Rincón, is the most rural part. Jiménez is the city. I called it, Jiménez is New York. Manhattan is there. So, when you talk there, their reality, even Marielos, that is, is very different from here. It's very different. They live off tourism and all that, but here no. People get around on horseback.",
  "CIT045": "- Catalina: Mami, what sonogram? Ultrasound, what ultrasound? Not at all, only now do I see all this fuss. They haven't been pregnant for even 15 days and they're already off to the clinic and looking for trouble. Everything hurts them already. And then, well, she gets better.",
  "CIT046": "- Catalina: Puerperal. Puerperal fever, and so they put me on a launch. Oh mamita, the melita that arrived - Eida: Oh, on the cart. - Catalina: The melita arrived at Salsipuedes, and they put me on that launch, mamita. That launch there, carrying bananas, carrying plantains at Sierpe, Cortés, all that. - Eida: And you in pain. - Catalina: Me dying with those high fevers. Well, so as not to tire you with the story, the launch broke down around midnight when it was passing Caño Island, and instead of going forward it went backward, backward. We came to dawn at Violín Island, we almost made it. Three days, four nights on that launch; they had to kill a pig because the food ran out, the water ran out, everything ran out, and me there on that launch until they turned it over for me.",
  "CIT047": "Catalina: If the woman gets hit, it crushes her inside faster. She comes and starts to get worse, ay, ay, ay. The next day, just a tiny one, the baby, they took it from (inaudible). The other one dies inside her. The child dies inside. The other day when they brought out Teresa, her belly was this big. Oh, woman of God, and there was no way to do anything, and every moment that passed the woman got more and more swollen, oh Lord, what do we do in this place, and I don't know what, so over there. At that time I had not yet seen any woman, well, the first one there was. Well, they make a hammock with two poles, one across over there and the other here, and they look for four people, each takes one end of a pole and another the other, and off to Río Oro.",
  "CIT048": "- Catalina: That woman caught fire, her granddaughter who was sitting beside her caught fire, and they run toward the Bajamar fleeing at a run, and the tide was out, dry, which was better. And the late Don Hugo behind them to throw a sack over them, a big cloak over them. And if they had waited, he throws that cloak over them and puts them out, because when you cover a person like that. Of course they would have been left with burns, they couldn't stand it.",
  "CIT049": "- Catalina: I was sick, I was already in pain from (inaudible) when she died. Don (inaudible) said, I'm going to go get, I always forget what her name is, I'm going to go get the midwife. Well, the thing is he went to look for the midwife and on the way he ran into a man who says to him, \"where are you going?\" He says, \"I'm going to look for Doña so-and-so, Toño's mother, because Cata is very ill and she had agreed that she was going to deliver her,\" and he says, \"where are you going to look for her? They are burying her already.\" And I hear it, and the chills grab me, and fever. Look, I didn't die of puerperal fever, really. It was a bad thing.",
  "CIT050": "- Catalina: I never went back. They took me out when the park came, I never went back. I have been to, what's it called? There at La Leona, yes there. Now I feel like going back in. - Rocío: You feel like it? - Catalina: Yes, but I tell them whoever takes me has to give me turtle eggs; I'm not going to go see the turtle eggs and not eat them. So you'll see which of you commits.",
  "CIT051": "- Catalina: What that place was like before. - Eida: But it was a paradise. - Catalina: And what a beautiful thing. Arriving at the mangrove and starting to gather pianguas, which looked like stones, all moss-covered. They even had silt on top. We would gather up heaps of pianguas. Those were the beans in those days; I would cook them with four coconuts in milk, washed. I would put them in, let that milk season them, like a Caribbean chicken, it would turn out like Caribbean chicken. Until there was nothing but pure oil, a big pot of pianguas. Make me that, make me that. The other time, the other time they served me the one from the jungle, I've already forgotten.",
  "CIT052": "- Catalina: Oh, if you had seen, if you had seen when we arrived there the first time, mamita. The neighbor, well, I already told you, the neighbor, I told you how many hours away the nearest one was, three hours' walk to San Perillo. We arrived there, mamita, the plane dropped us off, and what were we doing there at the table. We went to work with Fernando Araya, we called him (inaudible) and that man died. We went to work with him, month after month he would come to us to drop off the food. I thought, you know, to my husband something might have happened, where he would be beaten up all day by giant logs as tall as this house, something might have happened to him, what would I have done there, what enormous brutalities.",
  "CIT053": "- Catalina: Mami, all the time, as soon as one load ran out, imagine, in the house there was always one of those big drums full of dried meat. All kinds of meat. - Rocío: How did you dry the meat? - Catalina: Over fire. - Rocío: Over fire. - Catalina: You should have seen how delicious meat dried over fire was, (inaudible) you buy some pork, you make a drying rack, you slice it into strips, and you make a rack and put it, you set it to roast on little rods, they put rods all around. You lay banana leaves on it, you should see how delicious. Someday they'll have to come and... - Rocío: Do you still cook that way? Do you still cook that? - Catalina: I can still cook it. As long as there's someone to bring it to me.",
  "CIT054": "- Catalina: The stews of black iguana, with dried coconut milk, and the pianguas. The pianguas. There is no such iguana here, you only see it over there. That iguana you only see over there, they live in the big hollows of trees, that's all. But the most delicious thing there, listen to me, the curassows. When they were nice and fat, what a delicious thing! Or curassow soup, or a salpicón of tapir or white-lipped peccary, what a special thing! Nobody wants to commit to going to find me a loin of white-lipped peccary, much less bring me a curassow, you know.",
  "CIT055": "By the time I started going to school and everything, a lot of it had already been lifted, the restriction from the bank, from the Banco Nacional, there was a restriction there. That had already been lifted, by the time we moved to live there it had been eliminated. That was in the time, let me see, seventy, like in the sixties, sixty-five. At that time that restriction was still in place, that you had to ask for permission in order to go. For example, nothing, if you had to go and do something over there, right? And to go to the hospital, you had to go with the pass.",
  "CIT056": "There was a change of manager. So I don't know whether back then they were only gringos, but when the Latinos came. Well, I think it was a Honduran, a Honduran or a Panamanian. I don't remember right now, I think the surname was Romero, he came and did away with a lot of that. So it was gradually eliminated, they removed what they call the needles that were there at the bank.",
  "CIT057": "A \"conchero\" was the one who took the bunches of bananas. They didn't come in boxes yet. They came in bunches. So it was on the shoulder and carrying it to the ship. Quickly the company changed that system. So they started with what they call conveyor belts. Well, it's a pity, but it's there in the photos, where the belts went down to the ship. So only from the train, right? From the railcars, where the bananas came from the farms over there. So they passed them first as bunches, right? As bunches and passed them onto the conveyor belts. Then a change came, after I don't know how long, when they started to make the boxes here. They built a box factory.",
  "CIT058": "Well, sometimes the ones who visited were the people from Puerto Armuelles. Because they really liked chess and dominoes. And people here have always liked that a lot; in fact we still have a little group there, of the oldest ones, right? Old fellows there. Who are at the... well, in the park. In the Bellavista park, there is a little group that did... In fact that is what we want with the Ratino Club, to give them an opportunity to go and play. Because if the downpour comes right now. Well, by now they surely must have gone home, but in the afternoons if it rains they have to leave because they don't have a real roof, as there should be. Right? Or they'll all get wet, but groups like that still exist. Sometimes there were some here. Right at that school, just outside, they would organize there to play dominoes because they have always liked it.",
  "CIT059": "Not right now, it burned down. Someone burned it, we all know there is someone behind it, a shady hand. There, from the sports side, supposedly, that is what they mention, that they burned it on purpose. They did it deliberately, they had it burned. What for? To negotiate afterwards and see what would be done, and give it to certain people. I, for my part, I tell people here that I am still in the fight. And today I come a little bit happy, right? I had to fight a bit there, but I am happy because at least they have paid attention to us now. This new council, this new council, because they are only just about to complete a year.",
  "CIT060": "Take the school, for example. I was on the board of that school, when I joined the board. And a nun came there and told me, \"Gina, look, it's fine, let's go, let's go. But the day an earthquake comes to Golfito, nothing here is going to fall, nothing. What the company built, it built well.\" Ah, but somebody put a bug in the lady's ear that it was going to fall. So she comes to my house, because we live next door, and tells me, \"oh Doña Gina, I'm going to the Ministry of Health, the school is going to fall down.\" She was the principal, and I say, \"What?\" And she says, \"yes, it's that.\" Oh, dear sister, don't touch that, because it's a bone with ants. You don't know what you're going to touch, what you're going to do. Look, and no sooner said than done. She went to the Ministry of Health, and a month after going to the Ministry of Health, a man arrives there, an inspector, and tells us, \"I have bad news for you. Here, one month to vacate and one month to start tearing it down.\"",
  "CIT061": "I was a scout. I got into a bunch of things, as always; to this day I'm in associations, in cooperatives. Well, all of that because I love it. I like it, and I like politics, I like politics a lot, but well, that's how it goes here.",
  "CIT062": "La Papota was a restaurant, well, a dining hall, not a restaurant. No, a dining hall, which the company had for those night workers, because they were given good food. They had oatmeal, which was the most important thing, oatmeal in the early morning. Then the coffee, the gallo pinto, with plenty of scrambled eggs, and a breakfast and bread. Plenty of bread. And then meat, which they never lacked because it was brought from Puerto Jiménez, so meat was never missing. Right?",
  "CIT063": "The landing field, but as it was before. That is, the landing field is fine, there's no problem. It's the sides. The sides used to be a golf course. So, there are a lot of trees along the edges, coconut palms, no, not coconut. Apple trees, which in fact are in harvest now, apple and mango. That's it, you see, on the 9th of September, which is Children's Day, this school and the other school and all the schools would ask for permission, because you had to ask for permission, because there is a lot of responsibility, but mainly to notify civil aviation that the little ones were going there, the teachers took the little ones there. And it was lovely, because everything was like grass, like that. Everything, everything, everything, everything, everything, everything, you could see the orange grove. The orange grove, I almost started to cry. The day we went that day, where I saw the orange grove, and I saw all those bare trees, I thought, what are these people doing?",
  "CIT064": "I was born here in Golfito. On the 2nd of February, '55. So I'm 70 years old, well past it. My father was Nicaraguan. He was in Upala for a while. And as soon as the work started here, with the company, the rumors arrived, right? So he came here. To work here at the Golfito dock. And my mother is from San Ramón de Alajuela. And there my grandfather took them to Puntarenas. And from Puntarenas she came with her sister. Just girls, 18 and 19 years old. They came here, to Golfito. They started to work at the... At a... Well, what is burned down. I don't know if I can point at it, right? What is burned there. There was a hotel. It was called Hotel... Víquez. So upstairs, hotel. Downstairs was a restaurant, bar and store. So my mother worked there. My aunt didn't, because she was still very... And she suffered a lot from asthma. But my mother did. She worked there a long time. Later she met my father. They married. And that's it. They had the four of us. Four sisters. Four little girls.",
  "CIT065": "And it turns out I couldn't later, as soon as I started my degree I moved for work to the Judicial Branch; I was working first at the social security fund, just graduated, and then at the Judicial Branch. And at the Judicial Branch I was never going to stay on. I was on the first shortlist, but I wasn't eligible for the positions that were open because I had two more sisters, one in Pérez Zeledón and another in San José, working in the Judicial Branch. And back then no more than two relatives were allowed in the same institution. Not now. You see that in any institution there's the cousin, the aunt, the nephew, the grandchild, as many as fit in there, and they come in, right? But back then, no.",
  "CIT066": "The young women, well, I worked only with women. The groups of boys I taught were like a guidance class, nothing more. There were few lessons, but I did work with women. So they were girls who cared about learning, about knowing. Today I have, well, first I'm going to tell you that they labeled me as a kind of ogre, right? More than one of them, where I've seen her, would tell me I was very strict. And they were right. I was raised like that, I was educated like that, and I came and believed that was the right system. But I've had several former students from different groups who look me up. I still talk with some of them. At least once a year they come, we have a little coffee or they organize some activity and we share.",
  "CIT067": "I wasn't part of the banana company. My father never worked for the banana company. Rather, we enjoyed privileges that weren't even because of Dad's work, but were like a gift the banana company gave in order to have the authorities here, to impose order in the town, all that. But I imagine that for those who were employees and had to start as raw recruits, from the bottom up, it was very hard. It must have been very hard. To this day, when the banana company left, those who were in a good position bought the houses here in the American zone. Those who weren't bought them over in the parish neighborhood, which is where the Catholic church is. Those who weren't, in the gulf neighborhood that used to be called the gray zone. And those who weren't, at kilometer one, which was already the lowest category of workers there was in Golfito.",
  "CIT068": "And it turns out I couldn't later, as soon as I started my degree I moved for work to the Judicial Branch; I was working first at the social security fund, just graduated, and then at the Judicial Branch. And at the Judicial Branch I was never going to stay on. I was on the first shortlist, but I wasn't eligible for the positions that were open because I had two more sisters, one in Pérez Zeledón and another in San José, working in the Judicial Branch. And back then no more than two relatives were allowed in the same institution. Not now. You see that in any institution there's the cousin, the aunt, the nephew, the grandchild, as many as fit in there, and they come in, right? But back then, no.",
  "CIT069": "But it did work in that he didn't pay Social Security and didn't have to pay money toward his pension. He retired, he retired. Years later my father came out with a harebrained scheme, separated from Mom, got himself a young woman. The young woman gave him a daughter, and the thing is the young woman, by the time the girl was born, wasn't even a year in. When she left, she was Panamanian and went to Panama and left my dad raising the little girl. And to this day that little girl is more than 50 years old, or around 50, and still receives, lives off my father's pension. Unheard of too, right? But those are things that used to be in force, that worked in Costa Rica according to the regimes, and today no longer.",
  "CIT070": "Communism came in very strongly among the banana company's employees, and those who fought hardest for communism, defending the privileges of communism at the time, on behalf of the employees. In the end the banana company left. The employees lost everything they had, work, benefits and all of that. And, in contrast, those who came to defend the interests of the workers were the ones who ended up well placed in Golfito. Unheard of, huh? Because the principle is all for all and not all against all, right? But yes, there has been a lot of change. A lot of change. Unfortunately, the town of Golfito stayed, as we said yesterday, in that psychological-mental lethargy of depending on others doing everything for me, giving me everything, handing it to me.",
  "CIT071": "I remember my mother-in-law, whose husband, my father-in-law, was high-ranking here in the American zone. When they came to work here in Golfito, they came from Palmar, they came to Golfo. They had what is now UCR down there, where we were yesterday. What is that whole department called? It was the supermarket for the big shots of the banana company. You arrived and said, I need this, this, this, I'm taking this, this, and you didn't pay. So they were used to it. Who was going to worry about leaving something built for the future if they had everything at hand? They even gave them the dinnerware, the plates.",
  "CIT072": "Today I was talking with a sister who didn't live in the country, and she remembered precisely those times when there was... My father and mother were addicted to movies. At that time there were three movie theaters in Golfito. One at kilometer one, whose owner, and he still holds the license although he died years ago, was Don Luis Guachón, the one at kilometer one and the one at the golf course. The one at the golf course was the one in front of the stop for the boats to Cacao. All of that was also Teatro Guachón. And the other was La Bananera, where UCR now has the rooms upstairs and downstairs; back then the cinema was upstairs and downstairs was a restaurant where anyone who could pay for it could go. So they spent their time there perfectly well.",
  "CIT073": "All three. Dad went to all three. My dad and my mom were fanatics. They never missed going to the movies every night. Every night. So I, who was a little bit sharp, to use a nice word, would escape through the window of my house; we made a hole in the screen on the window; I'd drop from the roof of the house onto the Civil Guard's plaza, where you can still see the difference, and I'd land there and escape with my little brothers to go play at the Play, which is where it is today. They built some houses, but after the plaza. And we don't know how I had that agility. My little brother, the next one, yes, he was a boy and he was very artistic and did a lot of sports, but the youngest girl was a real pampered one. And it didn't matter, because going down you just let yourself drop from above, but how about going back up? I still don't understand it today. How did we climb up there? And we had to get to bed, right? No bathing, no putting on pajamas, we got under the blanket so Dad and Mom would think we had come from... that we were sleeping. And since there weren't many fans, we'd be dripping sweat, right? Muddy. And Dad and Mom never found out. Those are the anecdotes my siblings still hold against me.",
  "CIT074": "Because of Dad's work, he worked at the Judicial Branch. Back then there was no civil service. So every four years when the government changed, they'd transfer him to different places. We traveled through almost all of Costa Rica. And we were born in different places. I was born in Puntarenas, then he moved to Palmares. My second brother was born there, down the list. And the youngest was born in Quepos. And from Quepos we came to Golfito. I came to Golfito when I was about to turn nine, to do third grade. I was at the school and graduated from the Civil one, called Escuela Álvaro París. The school is still in the same place. I lived, or we lived, on the other side of the school plaza, which was my father's office and house. He was a judicial mayor, which today is a position something like a judge. Judicial judge.",
  "CIT075": "I love painting, all kinds of crafts, I like it a lot, so I came and started looking on YouTube and I saw some figures there, but I tried to find a way to make them much better than those, and I started putting one here and one there, and then I said, I'll see how, and I set aside space to put stones so people would respect it. People always came to dump trash on me. So I told them, please, I clean there so it looks clean, and so those stones I started hauling with my children, and when not, by myself, every time the tractor came to fix the roads. I took advantage of the stones they pulled up and started putting them all around the house, and I started making more figures to fill it with little animals, and I saw they turned out pretty, and I saw that people came and took photos, how pretty, and so on. Thank you, Lord, because what I wanted I am already accomplishing, since all of that is municipal land now. At any moment the municipality could come and tell me to remove all that, I'd have to take it away, but I've asked the municipal people, and they say, no, on the contrary, if only everyone were like you and did all of this so pretty. But, what's it called, it has cost me, because there are people who don't like cleanliness, don't like seeing things pretty or anything.",
  "CIT076": "When we were in school, the school was very nice, because in that childhood of before everything was very normal, there was no malice, none of that, everything was fun, going to the river, playing ball, there were women's teams, and there we played women against men. At school they gave us, the company sent us, always sent us, I don't know if it was monthly, a bag of milk with vitamins, and they also gave us cod liver oil pills. They gave us the notebooks and the books, that is, they gave us everything; we thought it was more than just going to study.",
  "CIT077": "My mother too, we are all from here, from Golfito, we were born here, we're here, and my brothers and sisters too. I'm a single woman, I have four children, I live here in Golfito, and I've always liked this gulf, even though when the company left, while it was here, I was always here, and thank God I took advantage of everything, everything that was the banana company, both here in the town of Golfito, the port of Golfito, and also the banana farms. Because my mother had to go find work there on the banana plantations, and she worked there, and so she took us there; we finished sixth grade, my sister and I, and she went to the high school in Ciudad Neily. My mother worked there until we grew up, by which time the company had already resigned, with so many problems from the strikes, the UTG, which was very insistent with this company, and the truth is that surely it ended. They got fed up with being in this mess, and these things, that they decided to leave.",
  "CIT078": "A \"brequero\" is the person, workers who got on the train, who climbed a ladder on top of the train and they walked, no matter how fast the train was going, they went walking and carried their, what's it called? Their lamp, that lamp that I carry. That lamp, they had their signal, whether for it to stop or so they would know there was going to be a curve or that they were going to switch tracks. Because there were these things there that, I don't remember what they're called, that you turned and the track shifted toward where the train was going to go, and when it was going to the other side, then the track shifted and went to the other side.",
  "CIT079": "So there were always trains every day, one for Palmar and another for Coto. And that train turned around way past Laurel, almost reaching Panama, the Panama side, and there it turned around and waited for people. People got on, and those who were going to play ball went to play ball, and we got as far as Coto 47, from there we got off and walked to the farm where we belonged, because Coto 47 was like the hub of all the Cotos. And, what's it called, and then later the train would turn back and come over here, around five, five, five-thirty that train would be arriving here in Golfito, and we got off at Coto 47 and walked from there, and if not, sometimes we were saved because maybe the man with the chapulín was dropping something off at that farm, and then we'd climb onto the chapulín. And there they took us, and since it was a road with a lot of stones, all of this that we were doing like that, but we went happily because we went along and that dust everywhere, because there is a lot of dust, because the road is not paved, there is far too much dust.",
  "CIT080": "So those little ones, those stamps like before, I don't know, their soul, anti-stain and none of that, only glue, and the glue wouldn't stick them. So I would take the little stamps to stick them, and not only me, afterwards everyone had the idea of sticking the stamps to cover notebooks, but I liked it. I have always liked having that collection of things from before, because time passes and they are things that maybe many people didn't see, didn't live, nothing, and maybe with that little bit, maybe it reminds them, or one reminds oneself how your childhood was, how your history was, how we lived it and how we felt it, and what's it called. So I've always liked that, I collect, I also have even cassettes there, which a lot of people have already thrown away, I have the, what do I remember it's called? Where you put the cassettes to listen to music, with Goldman, walkman it's called, I still have about two walkmans kept there, and I have one of the first ones that came out where you only sent the message and it captured it, and now cell phones came out, but those are no longer seen, they are things that very few people have.",
  "CIT081": "From the gate, from the bank over there. - Cecilia: From the hospital onward there was another guard too. It was stricter. Entering the American zone was stricter. Not everyone could go. - Lila: But the... what is it now? What's it called? Where there were dances and everything, what's it called? No, that was... - Cecilia: The Latin club. - Lila: No, their club, inside there. - Cecilia: Ah, the Club Centro. - Lila: The Club Centro, which is now UCR's. - Rocío: Did you go there? - Lila: No, they didn't let us in. - Cecilia: There was a bowling alley there, they showed movies and there was a bar too. - Rocío: And why didn't they let you in? - Lila: Because you had to have permission. - Cecilia: Because it was mainly for the company's employees and we... - Lila: For the high-level employees, not all of them. For the high-level ones, not the rank and file. - Cecilia: The rank and file couldn't enter.",
  "CIT082": "Nothing, the same people. This is a town of friends, as they say. We all knew each other. Even the bosses were also the... They were customers of the restaurant and all that. I do remember. Since there were all kinds of wines and liquor and all that, they drank. There was a lady who supplied them their meals, but she took them from the restaurant here and brought food to the club. Because she lived there where it is now... Well, they called it the beach house, but all of that belonged to the company, where the bachelors lived, they were bachelors, as they called them, right? And now it's... Yes, all of it is... Now almost the majority of that belongs to UCR. Yes, where all of that was. There was also a... There was a... What did they call it? The supermarket that had everything.",
  "CIT083": "I had little Ceci. And we were born here in the Golfito hospital. And afterwards... With her father. He worked for the company in Coto. So I went with him to Coto. I was in Coto 52 and in Coto 47. There the company only had... For transport it was the train. We went by train. They took everything there... Everything they needed in Coto, up to... And in those years there was no hospital, only a dispensary. Like, say, the EBAIS now. So I would take her, little as she was, to have her checked.",
  "CIT084": "And other memories I have from my childhood. It's when the company fumigated the houses. In that era they fumigated us with DDT. We're alive by sheer miracle. We're alive. They would tell us, vacate the houses. And for us it was a day out in the country. Because my grandmothers would take out a brazier, a wood stove. Well, at that time they cooked with firewood. The banana company had, since the houses were the company's property, big iron stoves. And they themselves came around distributing the firewood. And each family chopped it. And had their... how do you say it? When they stack it. They lay it out in the sun and everything. And that day when they fumigated. Which I remember very well. For us it was a day out in the country. Because we ate outside the house. In the yard. And they let us go back in only in the dead of night. When the poison had dissipated a bit. And the walls were left white. Completely coated with DDT.",
  "CIT085": "When I was about, years, two years old. That was when Mom came to Golfito. So they brought me there. From there, and I grew up here in Golfito. When the company was at its height. I have memories of when we traveled by train. There was no road. This road that goes to Río Claro didn't exist. It was a track. And so the rivers, you had to cross them like that. Into the river. Throw yourself into the river, as they say. And we traveled by train to the farm. Sundays were the trips. For us, being kids, it was wonderful to travel by train. And those are the memories I have of my childhood. Of when we traveled by train. When the company was here. The banana company. We lived in a hamlet called kilometer one.",
  "CIT086": "So, I remember that I traveled by motor-car with a priest whose surname was... No, whose first name was Father Félix. Father Félix. So we were a group of girls, about 10, 12 years old. Yes, because I was 12 years old, barely. They took us to Coto when he went to say Mass. For us it was a lovely outing. And not everyone had that privilege of traveling by motor-car. Which held about 5 people. The loveliest thing was traveling by motor-car. And we also traveled by train to take the Sunday outings. On Sundays we traveled by train to the farm. So that was our entertainment that there was here. Because there was nothing here. Besides that. It was very heavy, stitched, so one didn't even do it. Because the grown-ups did that. But yes, that was very beautiful. It was a very wholesome childhood. At that time it was very wholesome. And what I remember was a girl... Mine was a lovely girlhood. Lovely here in Golfito. With my family, my brothers and sisters. My first four siblings were there. Three brothers.",
  "CIT087": "Ismael: Well, a man came who was a Cuban. And he fixed it. The thing is he kept throwing a lot of earth over this way. So I stood up and told him no. That he should leave it to me. Because they were doing... They were throwing all the earth over here. - Rocío: Who does the plaza belong to today? - Ismael: That belonged to the Ureñas. There's a plan that Doña Juana and the late Rubén had. The two old folks. A big plan they had. So I bought, and it cost me to get the deed because of that. But a man came whom they called Figueres. José María Figueres. And I tell him... If you... If you give me... If you give me the deed here. I'll sign and I'll go for you, and my family. They'll all go so that you become president. Not president, of... - Rocío: President, yes - Ismael: Yes, yes. Ah, yes. The president. So I tell him... If you become president I... I'll do what I can, but only if you give me the deed. And that's how it was. - Rocío: And why does he give him the deed? - Ismael: Because legally, as he was already president, then. He came and spoke with the people and. Today we started to measure. The MINAE and all of that, we measured and everything, and when he noticed, the deed came to me. For everything. And so the plaza was left separate, and the power station, the school and the church. Everything was left separate.",
  "CIT088": "Rocío: And how did they build the houses on the ranches? With what... How did they make them? - Ismael: Ah, with saws. A saw, and they started to cut. By the way, I brought the saws, I carried them from my place. And the people were glad, and we started cutting lumber. And we made all the little houses. Of pure wood. We made them from a wood they called Aguacatón and Amarillón. And well, the things we said, the little houses then.",
  "CIT089": "And a man drew me a plan. To build the house. But a brother-in-law of mine comes, and he lived over in Buenos Aires. And he tells me, \"look, dude, why don't you do me the favor and give me the little job? Because I'm in a bad way, bad.\" Very poor. And to do him a favor I gave him work. And he comes and we started cutting lumber, the way I tell you. One lives, I work. Until we finished cutting the lumber. And when it was cut I say, this much is sure. We're going to build it with two floors. And I took out lumber of 4x6 timber. And I built it. Look, it's very pretty. We were making the house very pretty. But I left, when it was just beginning, I went to get everything. Because when I got there it was hard. Everything had to be brought by horse. The zinc and the wire and nails. Well, everything. Here there was only wood. Only that. And so I went over there, and when I get back here. And the little ones, they didn't know anything, when I arrived the one who was building the house for me, he was going to build the house. Not on floors but on bases. Those bases that go on the ground. Small like that. When I arrive they had set those posts for me. And I almost died. Seeing that, and how I was going to get the lumber out again. Which was costly. So well, let's do it like this, but well done. A single floor. And so we started building it, but when he built that for me, here come the motorbikes. And he comes when it was already finished. It was already, well, it was all gone to hell.",
  "CIT090": "- Felipe: What do you think of Rancho Quemado now? How has the town changed? - Ismael: Ah, it has changed enormously. And here there was no school. Here the schools... They were all in bad shape... But... Thank God a grandson of mine is an engineer. The one I tell you is the son of Alicia who lives in Quepos, and so he comes and he was the one who took charge of fixing this road and these bridges and all that. And like him, no, because the lawyers, the mayors are middling. So there was one they called Alberto Coles, so he was middling, so he comes and then that mayor, Don Ney Gamboa, is very good, very expert in that. And so he helps us build these roads, but it's that you... Because you were... We used to go there... To Jiménez... And when we came back there were the creeks, and they would take us along... Like that with... It does nothing... The most, about fifteen years... Ten years... And now thank God to join up well and... For all that... That young man... Helped us a lot...",
  "CIT091": "I feel very happy, it's just that we were able to plant Palma Tica, and that has done us a lot of harm. The first was at a hundred, a hundred and ten per ton. And then it went to thirty-five. Now it's more expensive, but we had already bought a quantity. Now it's more expensive, but we can't even buy enough to fertilize it properly. Because when I did it they gave things in such quantities it scared me, they even gave us a hundred sacks on board for us to put on the... - Rocío: Who gave you that? - Ismael: It's Palmatica... So that we would plant... We got enthusiastic and gave ourselves to it until... But thank God we've paid. We paid, and now for Palma Tica we only give them the little palm shoots they used to give us. We've paid the rest already...",
  "CIT092": "And he says, Trino says, \"well, if you wait for me, give me a boot and I'll go get water.\" One of those boots one wears. In the rubber thickets. And he took that boot and went, and washed it a little, and brought it back and sticks to the boot. Those who were there, Chepón and Jeremías. The thing is... That Chile has already passed. All that already... Thank God that already...",
  "CIT093": "There was nobody here. There was no one at all. Only the Ureñas were here, nobody else, and me when I arrived. And when I arrived it was the Guardian Angel, because I began helping them with the roads. With this road. This road from here to Banega we made on this side and it was no use. So I say, \"look, let's make a deal, I'll cut a little trail and you follow behind. I know that this way we come out on flat ground.\" And off we went, we went, we went. And we arrived where there was a slope, but it was already nicer. And this road that we have now. Thank God. And so we kept forcing it through until one day I went to the... I had a friend they called... Who was Víctor... From some people... He wanted to be a deputy. And he said, \"if I win, I'll help you make the road.\" So this one did win. So he came and then he gave me...",
  "CIT094": "And when I arrived that house was there. That little burned ranch. And there was a man. And he tells me, I want a little piece of meat. From wild peccary. And I say, fine. I say, I'm hungry. And he says, fine. With a bit of banana. Cooked banana. And I say, and how did this ranch burn down? And we went over there. And we left this badly set up. And when we arrived it had burned. And we were done for. But now we're seeing how we make a living.",
  "CIT095": "And we went back. They showed the animals and we went back. When we arrived there was no cattle at all. If that cattle had gone back, we didn't know what to do with that cattle. And I say, look, let's make a deal. Let's sleep here in the middle of the road. When they wouldn't stop, Carlos. Since the road was in bad shape, there were no cars. I say, let's put the packs and the animals here and sleep here. In the morning I'll go and you stay looking for the cattle.",
  "CIT096": "When there, going down, I saw a granadilla vine. Ripe ones, the kind that are very delicious. And I was so hungry and so weak and so anguished that I drank about four or five. I made them, I drank them. I knew they were good, that they weren't poisonous. Some granadillas that are kind of speckled. Well, thank God now I'm full. Then I see a chapulín coming down there, heading to Chacarita. And I shouted and it stopped. There were very good people. And it stopped and I say to him, \"why don't you take me?\" I say, I'm tired, and, \"yes, of course, of course, climb on here.\" He took me and I got on there.",
  "CIT097": "And there I was at night, around seven at night, with the cattle. So I said, and there. I thought to myself, everything came to me and I hadn't brought a horse because it didn't fit in the car. It didn't fit. So when I saw, he tells me, I say, couldn't you lend me an animal? And he says, well, there is an animal that has barely been ridden twice, but if you want, we'll get it for you so you can go. I say, that's fine. Since I lived in the countryside, I rode animals. I got on, it bucked three times and then it stayed quite still. It came along.",
  "CIT098": "I arrived, two and a half hours later I got to where the Ureñas were. Jeremías and Trino and one they call Chepón, who was their brother-in-law. Yes, I think those were the ones who were there. When I arrived, they were so happy to see me there. And there, what happened to you? He says, what happened to you? I say, can't you see what happened to us. I got there at ten at night, at almost eleven. And on foot, listen, from Chacarita.",
  "CIT099": "Rocío: But tell me something... We saw there was a sort of film that had been made, did they never show you anything? Ismael: No... Rocío: I mean, they didn't... They didn't show you... They never showed you a film about the town? Ismael: No, no, no... (Page 10) Ismael: Ah, yes, yes... Only that... A daughter of Félix came... Who was the... The one who interviewed... She was the one who... Translated for him... Yes... Since she knew me there... All that... And so when... He finished going there... The surprises came to me... All is good...",
  "CIT100": "After 5 days my brother Luis arrived and tells me, I've come to help you build a ranch. Oh, what a relief. So we worked, we cut wood and put up this ranch. We put up the ranch, cut leaves and thatched it. Then we moved into the ranch to live, we no longer slept in the (inaudible) of the chonta palm. Life has changed. It's not the same to sleep in the (inaudible) of the chonta palm as to sleep in a (inaudible). And that's how we moved on. Years later, I built a wooden house. A man showed up with a portable saw and sawed some lumber for me, and I built a wooden house. We lasted many years there. After many years, I built this house. And this house is 35 years old. That's how old this house is. I have never changed my way of living. Here were born, I have 10 children, 6 girls and 4 boys.",
  "CIT101": "You know, to go into a faraway place, leaving your mother and everything, and see something so hard. We didn't bring deeds, (inaudible). And to go, at least, getting ahead of the highlight, so you know, here we spent six months without tasting even coffee or sugar. Eat game meat, and wild animals, and water from the creeks; with that we sustained ourselves. And we worked from five to five, swinging machete and axe, and after five we had to wash, cook and do everything that had to be done, because there was nobody else here in the mountains.",
  "CIT102": "Five years, all alone here. We were all single. All alone, I tell you, working, cooking and washing. For us to eat a \"cuadrado\", you know what a cuadrado is, right? We had to go through these mountains, and we opened a trail to Drake, or to Estero de Guerra, to bring whatever we could carry on our backs, so we could have cuadrados, to eat with meat. I started hauling timber. Ah, well, after five years we already had banana groves here, plantain groves, bean fields, beans by the ton, corn. After five years, I brought this woman here. We met there and I talked. And what did I have to tell her about marriage? No, here we didn't know a priest, here we didn't know anything. You had to state the need there was, why did I need a woman? Not one need, there were several. One was, first of all, companionship. Second, she's a cook. The clothes have to be washed; we had to wash our clothes at night, however tired we arrived. And so it was a need. It wasn't so much, maybe, everything is included, but it wasn't as perfect as the need for everything that covered that. Well, when I told her, I told her, \"look, if you go with me, you go, and if not, no hard feelings, nothing will happen.\"",
  "CIT103": "He says to me, I remember, who were you on that day? Of course, what he's looking at is that in those times here there was no money, here there were no roads, here there was nothing, everything had to be done by sheer strength and will. And it was done, and it got done. But I lasted here 20 years, and I would tell people, \"look, we have to go open a ditch like this, like this, like this, to set the culvert.\" Twelve men would show up at dawn, ready, with lunches, with pick and shovel and everything, and off we went. At 5 in the morning, and at 7 at night we'd be coming back. All the time, 12, 14 men. People never asked me, how much are you going to pay? No, here we didn't talk about money. Here it was said that we had to work and we worked, all of us. And now here there is an ease, well, say for the last 15 years, since Jessica stepped in and I left the boards and handed it over to her. But now there are facilities, now there is electricity, now there are water systems, now there are roads, now there are halls, now there are churches, now there are schools. Did you see the school? And that is another story.",
  "CIT104": "Because I was an incorrigible drunk, I would go to Sierpe and last 20 days lying on the sidewalks. At midnight I would get up at 1 in the morning and drink the dirty water that ran out of the washbasins in Sierpe. And I would last 20 days without coming here, already married. Well, one day a man arrived here, a companion, who had been a drinking companion and who had left here for Barranca de Puntarenas. And he arrived and says to me, well, we had breakfast together and he says, look, \"how are you feeling? Are you well?\" I say to him, \"fine.\" That was what they gave him, 8 or 10 days. I had 8, 10, 12 gallons of moonshine ordered for Christmas Eve and New Year. That is what I answered him that day. But he told me, you don't look very well. But I was surprised, because I saw he looked very well dressed, healthy, everything. And I say to him, \"Mario, what do you eat that you're so healthy?\" \"Look, no, I eat the same, except I eat it without liquor.\" I say, \"how?\" \"Yes, I quit drinking. I quit drinking two years ago when I left here. I left and got stuck in a binge.\" He was a bricklayer, a very good bricklayer. \"I went into the bar and started drinking, a whole day and a whole night.\" In Barranca there are lots of people. Far too many people in those streets. \"And there I drank so much liquor, the day and the night, I woke up blind drunk and I felt like going out into the street around 12 noon and I went out\" like that, I'm going to say it in the words he told me. \"I went out and I shat in the middle of the street and I ate the shit.\" In front of everybody. \"And I fell there, there I am fallen. When a man appeared in a car, a big car, and me fallen there, shat on, pissed on, vomited on, and he said to me, come, get in here so I can take you home. I couldn't stand my own stench, and I see this car is brand new and the man was telling me I had to take it, and he said, come, come, and since I didn't go, he grabbed me and took me. But he took me, and told me it was for home, but no; when I arrived, I arrived and some women come out and some men who are not from my house come out and I stay, and those women greeted me, hugged me, took me, gave me soap, gave me shampoo, gave me clothes, bathed me, they put me at a table, brought me coffee, brought me chocolate, brought me empanadas, everything. Blessed God, what is this. I am in a strange place and this is something that has never been done for me at home. And there, they told me that this was an Alcoholics Anonymous hall.\" And imagine this, this was a while ago, that I hadn't even heard of Alcoholics Anonymous until that day when he is telling me what Alcoholics Anonymous is.",
  "CIT105": "Four boys and six girls. Okay. When I had the ranch, I had a ranch there, we had already opened a trail from Drake to Rincón. And there was a family that lived in San Josecito, which had family in Cañada. So, with a road there now, they came on foot. A woman with six kids. They came from over there, there was nothing here. It was the only station there was. There was no other house here. They stayed here, slept here that night, ate with us, and the next day they continued on. They would be eight days over there, and the same ones would come back. They came from there, another night here, and continued the next day. This lasted for many years. I don't know if you know them, they are the Talenos of Drake. Look, there are things it's good to mix in, so you realize how life has been lived. Taleno, Carlos Taleno, who is the eldest, I'm telling you, there were seven of them (inaudible). But since they arrived here, I had a pile of meat there, they ate meat. And plantains or bananas, the thing is they filled themselves up and slept. They became very fond of me. He is a heavy drinker and a marijuana smoker. But one of those marijuana smokers, you know, who if there's none, doesn't speak to anyone. Look, and at the age I am now, I run into Carlos Taleno in Drake, whether there are people or not, and he is lost in drugs, and he lifts his eyes and opens them like this, he knows me, opens his eyes, hugs me, he fills up like that, hugs me. \"Wow, my dad,\" he says to me, \"where did I come to find you, what a joy.\" He hangs on me from here, like the first day. They remember that. Their mother died a year ago. I mean, what I want to say is that memories remain of those friendships.",
  "CIT106": "Now then, I'm going to say this right now. I never needed marijuana, I never needed drugs, absolutely, absolutely. I have lived off what God has given me, and may God let me produce in a healthy way. I worked. There is something mysterious in me. I never studied. What I studied was 3 months of first grade at a school, nothing more. And with this, listen to what I'm going to tell you, whether there are virtues or there aren't. I'm telling you, I didn't study. I, well, first of all, am the founder of this town. I started when the first school was built. Well, a miracle of God. A miracle of God. That is how I see it. We have a Catholic church that is a whole story. They stole my money. Blessed God. I didn't give the devil his satisfaction. They stole my money. A neighbor himself. I already suffer from diabetes and when I saw that, my sugar went up almost to normal. But I had cattle here. So I told my daughter, I won't give the devil his satisfaction. I'm going to sell the cattle and we'll build the church. Whoever can't stay, I say, (inaudible). Blessed be the cattle. And we paid for the church.",
  "CIT107": "But here to survive many dogs were used, not one, up to 15. And several of them were doing a job, in there, and when they were coming back, the dogs picked up a scent there, they got into big trouble there with an animal, they were just kids, a big trouble with an animal, and that animal was enormous. I wasn't there, I was at the house, it was a giant anteater. - Rocío: What's a \"horse bear\"? - Delfín: A \"horse bear\" is an enormous animal, it weighs a few hundredweight, and it has an enormous tail, so when it gets angry it stands up like a person, and runs like a person, after the dogs, with its tail over its back, so it is such a monster. But they don't do anything, they have no teeth, they only have the nose, the snouts, long like that, to stick out the tongue to eat leafcutter ants and termites, things like that.",
  "CIT109": "But, blessed be God, we have come to understand all that, that if you go today to plant crops in those areas that were destroyed needlessly, not done, because the land burned the entire productive area for you, everything burned. And now you see everything differently, you go here, there are businesses, there are plazas, even with street lighting. It is true that many institutions have helped us, but also, you can see for the most part, that we depend on very hardworking families, an enormous number of hardworking people. Yes, just like that.",
  "CIT110": "We ate healthy, all our lives. Palm heart, wild game, rice, beans, cassava, quichamol, taro, papaya, soursop, pineapple, everything God gives you to live happily. And that gave me powerful health. Under that beautiful story that I tell you, I had a problem with alcoholism.",
  "CIT111": "Hunting has been eliminated, then among the institutions that supported the overturning, all of that has come, it is now paralyzed, it is now paralyzed. People have shaped themselves in different ways. There are areas that have been reforested by an institution called Boscosa. It had very good intentions for the environment, and gave out some small payments to help the farmers reforest areas.",
  "CIT112": "And also this had already been rented out for Melina, some years ago, about 30 years ago. And Melina is the true home of the leafcutter ant, they eat that. So these became leafcutter ant colonies of hectares, and then the companies finished with the areas, they left, and the farmers came back and turned them into cattle areas, who knows what. So the leafcutters left the nurseries, their homes, and then they attack almost everything. If you plant papayas, orange trees, they eat everything. And they kill it because they eat today and finish off the crown the tree had and leave it, they go to another place and come back when it has regrown, and it comes, so they become leafcutters, they are leafcutters. All those things, and also scarlet macaws and parrots have multiplied a lot, or there are plenty, and they eat the orange, the mandarin, the mamón, anything that is fruit they do eat. And since there are so many, they arrive and it's not that they even eat it, they tear it off and if they taste it and it's not ripe they throw it away, so yes, they ruin the harvest.",
  "CIT113": "\"Unfortunately Satan is the true promoter of there being in a drink of liquor such a harmful and destructive force that undoes and shatters a whole family and society. That is the problem, the root of the whole problem is in Satan.\"",
  "CIT114": "The forestry company absolutely prohibited it, and they had a command, they made visits, you saw those that were abandoned there, that have already rotted; with those they flew over all this, and had people ordered to the ground. They sent back the rice, beans, sugar, salt, everything, just so they couldn't use it, couples with families who had already settled in Playa Blanca and those areas over here, so they couldn't eat. They took away the knife and the axe, they took the father away under arrest. I mean, they were years of hard battle. With that company a hope of a road never came, never. They had yachts, they had airplanes, they had helicopters, they had ships, they exported the timber to Europe. I mean, they did everything and had all the powers in their hands, because the government that led the country in those days was Liberación, and they were the ones who gave them that concession. Thank God Rodrigo Carazo Odio came, for the National Unification Party, won the elections, and he expropriated that company.",
  "CIT115": "When I came here I was reestablished within the family. I was reestablished. And so the eldest sister. Her husband dies, an old man. And a son of hers is killed in an accident. And she is left with only one, who is this one, Enrique. And since that place is already wooded. There are many animals and I lived very much alone. She couldn't keep herself up, that's why Enrique, and with their help. They built this little house for her. She was here a short while ago, now she is with our eldest brother. Here in the very center. She lives right in front of the school. She likes to be there because they are the two eldest siblings. And she likes to talk a lot. So people go there and talk. And here very few come. Only select ones. Yes, yes. So she told me. She told me, if you want, go over there. You stay there and I'm over there.",
  "CIT116": "- Delfín: No, no. This sister of mine, a sister down here, and all alone. And she is very afraid of hurricanes, earthquakes and so on. And so I come at five and I stay with her. I stay, and at four-thirty I go over to where she is.",
  "CIT117": "Today, at sixty-five years old, my father and my mother have already died, and three brothers. None of them died as an older adult, not as old folks. Quite strong, solid, like I am. My mother too, at eighty, my mother is very strong, she was still washing, doing everything. My father, from so much work, got throat cancer and that did end his life in about two years. He was about sixty-six. But the rest, the whole story I tell you has been a story of a great deal of walking.",
  "CIT118": "I say it because I was never a hunter, never. My brothers were hunters, but I went with them to haul the dead animals, to roast them, to eat, because that is what there is to eat. But killing a monkey because it eats your corn, say, is a mistake, it is like killing a child of a family, it is the same for them, the same, the same. The fury and the fight of a band of monkeys, when a monkey is lying there alive and they can't do anything, it is hours of struggle and they can't do anything. Any human being can do that, we all see that, but in the times I grew up, all of that, for a cornfield we planted, if we had to shoot 100 monkeys, we shot them, and in the end they ate the cornfield anyway, because it wasn't 100, 300 came afterward. All of that is part of what I have seen in life.",
  "CIT119": "- Delfín: No, I've already explained to you the time I lost. And it also has to be made the most of. And today that I'm here with you, I recognize that time is very valuable, but that we have no comparison. So if one day we are left with an opportunity, and we have had difficulties in life, and that day is the best, we have to make the most of it. Perfect.",
  "CIT120": "And it turns out there was the uproar of 20 dogs there, just imagine. My brother went in without even knowing what the problem could be. First he thought it was surely the jaguar, but then they saw it running all over the place, and the shot killed it. The claws of those creatures are like this, just two, one on each hand, and that's how they stay, two, and then the other small ones. That, I never forget, I was about 22 years old. It is one of the biggest mistakes of one's life, with a firearm. That creature did nothing, nothing. You can walk by, look, you can pass a meter from it, and if it is full of termites, maybe you'll see it again, but it won't kill you. It is one of the most peaceful animals there are.",
  "CIT121": "When I came here, I got to know the bridges. At twelve. I got to know the bridges of El Brujo and Palmar Norte. They came by car, so that's how I got to know them. The bridges on the highway. I didn't know them before. Imagine, that, yes, that stays engraved in me for the whole story, I'd say.",
  "CIT122": "- Rocío: Why do they get sick? Sorry? What kinds of pests are there? - Delfín: They turn yellow and it seems, other technicians have come here and told us that the soil here lacks a lot of calcium and potassium, phosphorus, in some areas, and that keeps the plant from developing as well as it should, so some pests make them sick very easily, they don't have much defense. Also now, with an environment fuller of forest, because there is a lot of forest, many animals come. Now, at the 75 years I am, if I plant cassava where I am, the animals eat the plant. If I plant chamorro, tiquisque, and the herd of white-lipped peccaries that come down here from the park, the group of ranger-monitors who work here control them and send them back, but if they get through, they love it, so they stay there a few weeks until they've eaten it. If they're happy, that is their food.",
  "CIT123": "I say it because I was never a hunter, never. My brothers were hunters, but I went with them to haul the dead animals, to roast them, to eat, because that is what there is to eat. But killing a monkey because it eats your corn, say, is a mistake, it is like killing a child of a family, it is the same for them, the same, the same. The fury and the fight of a band of monkeys, when a monkey is lying there alive and they can't do anything, it is hours of struggle and they can't do anything. Any human being can do that, we all see that, but in the times I grew up, all of that, for a cornfield we planted, if we had to shoot 100 monkeys, we shot them, and in the end they ate the cornfield anyway, because it wasn't 100, 300 came afterward. All of that is part of what I have seen in life.",
  "CIT124": "Delfín: Of course, I mean I'm a farmer. I bring plantain, like here, I think anything. I have always produced, I have always produced. Ever since I grew up, all my life, my family has been farmers. - Rocío: How did you learn? - Delfín: My parents taught us, my parents had sugarcane fields, coffee plantations. By the time we grew up, we used that only a little, just for expenses or some small thing, but yes, we always produced the rice, the beans and everything we ate, cassava, plantain, every kind of tuber, everything, all fruit, papaya, avocado, all that, we produced it to eat. Now there are many diseases and that makes the trees get very sick, they hardly produce.",
  "CIT125": "Mom sent us to grind corn, me and Trino, at the creek. There were the biggest stones in all of human existence. For them to grind things. There we ground the corn. And there were three stones, for little Indians like us, when we were seven years old. For young people and also for adults. They were three tombs, stones from big tombs. Like one, here there is one of those. The biggest one is one of those. And then there is also a smaller one. There we ground the corn to make chilate. To make everything, and the coffee. On that stone. As I remember, the whole time we lived over there, we did all that there. On that Indigenous tomb. And when I came here, when I was already about twelve years old, we were scarce.",
  "CIT126": "Delfín: No, that, yes, Mom, Mom, I don't remember ever having clay jars, no. We used gourd bowls to eat. And to spoon the rice. And to stir the silate too, spoons made from calabash, from gourds, a long fruit like that. That there was here. That's what we used, those gourd bowls to eat. Soup, rice, anything. There was no soap. Mom, from tallow, from cow, they bought a flour, a powder they called potash. It still exists. And you add the soap. And then they prepared it with a plant called jaboncillo. Jaboncillo is a blue, slimy plant. So they mixed that and boiled it all together. And then they took out the pot of that, let's say a soap, of that thing. And from there they took that out and with that the clothes were washed. There wasn't any kind, as I remember, Mom never, I didn't know store-bought soap. When I was already at that age. Here, yes, here she did buy blue soap. That was already, already another era. When we came here, when I came, there were a lot of Indigenous signs here, many huacas.",
  "CIT127": "We went along those ridges until we came down to a place where we found a trail and footprints of people and took a creek downhill. But that is far from here. And when we were going there we saw a track and a sandbank at a bend in a creek. A little sandbank. We said, people live here, but no way. We continued and the people had already left. They had surely come to hunt, who knows. The thing was we kept going forward, and kept at it until we reached where there was a neighbor. It was a Nicaraguan who was there, with whom, by the way, from there on we became friends, and he helped us a lot because he was very poor but he had plantain groves and he had pigs.",
  "CIT128": "Dogs, when they are good, when they like to go in the mountains, for example, an animal passes, leaves its track and its scent, you set the dog on that track and he goes off in a single howl behind it. They have a searching howl behind the animal. When they reach the animal, they change their bark, louder, more joyful. So that was what got you excited, because the dog runs the animal, so you cross over with the gun and where the animal passes, you shoot it.",
  "CIT129": "- Trino: We didn't, when we built, we put up a little ranch with four posts, like a ranch, say like a house to climb up and sleep. The ground-rod one was made by the gold prospectors. - Felipe: With a thatched roof? - Trino: With a thatched roof. - Felipe: And did they use some kind of special wood? - Trino: No, any rod that gave a nice thickness to put, that was neither thick nor thin, just solid. - Felipe: And did they sleep on the platform or on the ground? - Trino: Those people slept, some slept on the ground, others made a cot there, they pounded a chonta palm, do you know what a chonta is? They pounded it, because even that was an experience for me, I didn't know it; we slept on the ground but already in a ranch, and there, those people from Guerra, no. They felled the chonta and took an axe to it and began to chop it very densely and then they struck it with the eye of the axe and made it pounded, and split it and opened it, and it came out as one of those lovely, soft things. And then they made a little bed on forked posts, laid small sticks across, and mounted the chonta there, and slept on top of that pounded chonta.",
  "CIT130": "- Felipe: When you arrived, the whole family, which was fourteen, you came from Volcán, and you used a term that was, say, that this land was not demarcated. - Trino: It belonged to no one, we say we \"carrilamos\" it. \"Carril\" is enclosing what we claim. - Felipe: And how did you do that? Putting up a fence. - Trino: No, swinging machetes, making a lane beneath the forest, laying out all the fine detail, and it looks like clearing a street. - Felipe: And did you have to maintain that carril? - Trino: It has to be cleaned once a year. - Felipe: The 3000 hectares? - Trino: Yes, at first it was 3000, but later we left it at roughly between 800 and 1000 hectares here. - Felipe: And that was already yours? - Trino: Yes, we brought an engineer and measured it. At that time the survey plan was valid, but when IESCO and all the IDA institutions started to come in, that plan was no longer valid, because it was a single one covering everyone's land, not farm by farm, no. What they call a mother plan. Once the IDA stepped in, we began to measure each one's own lot, more or less about 80 hectares each.",
  "CIT131": "Also here, thank God, we have managed a little rural tourism. So we devote ourselves to making trails and finding waterfalls. The lagoons. And to taking care of the animals, which is what we do most. We take care. As long as people don't kill the animals like the wild peccaries. We looked after them day and night. So that, say, in order to get people to protect them too. And thank God we managed it. Because the pure truth is that here in Rancho Quemado we achieved it.",
  "CIT132": "Happy. Here everyone has everything, pure life. We are all friends. We all work for the community. For oneself, personally. Receiving people from everywhere. We have places to have fun walking in the mountains. For many years we hunted, we felled forest, but there came a moment when we stopped. We never did it again. We decided not to hunt anymore. Not to fell more forest. And rather we have served as an example for many people.",
  "CIT133": "- Trino: Very exciting. Well, the other thing is that we took care of them, and that is where we did the numbers to see how to make sure that whoever joined really was a person who committed, because we had many people in there, but since they were hunters they were still fresh, they didn't do anything, they had been killing animals. There were some who wanted to get to know the spots where the animals were, so we did watch over that, to see that the people were really committed to what we were doing. So we began to go around everywhere to watch over the animals. We had knowledge, or have, right, that we would say the animals have to be in such-and-such a place, we have to go see whether there are people hunting them, and we would go and arrive where the animals were, and that's how we worked.",
  "CIT134": "- Trino: Firearms. Yes, so here you would see anybody pass by with a gun on the shoulder, and there was no law, we didn't have the group; that was when the group started, right? So, we began telling them, yes, we published that if we saw anyone with a firearm we would report them. Listen, we now have four years that we haven't seen anyone with a firearm again. That's a lot; just by not carrying firearms the animals aren't killed. The dogs, you should have seen how many hunting dogs people had; when people saw that we were working seriously they sold the dogs, many dogs died by now, and we are content, right? Because there were no firearms anymore, and if someone had one, they had it hidden so that we wouldn't see it. We began to work. We spent a little while, when they set up the MINAE post, we were saying to put it there so they could help us, but there was a chief, I can't remember his name right now. He was in Puerto Jiménez and he had forbidden the park rangers he had here, there were quite a few, from playing ball so they wouldn't mix with us, and from talking with us, so they wouldn't rub shoulders. So we were in a bad way with that, because then we weren't doing anything, they worked separately and we worked separately, and when they began to see that we were working the way we were working, we convinced them, we convinced them.",
  "CIT135": "Because there were no firearms anymore, and if someone had one, they had it hidden so that we wouldn't see it. We began to work. We spent a little while, when they set up the MINAE post, we were saying to put it there so they could help us, but there was a chief, I can't remember his name right now. He was in Puerto Jiménez and he had forbidden the park rangers he had here, there were quite a few, from playing ball so they wouldn't mix with us, and from talking with us, so they wouldn't rub shoulders. So we were in a bad way with that, because then we weren't doing anything, they worked separately and we worked separately, and when they began to see that we were working the way we were working, we convinced them, we convinced them.",
  "CIT136": "So the problem exists, right now that problem exists, so their job is that, going around confiscating sawn timber, which I say they should rather go out, make trips everywhere, before they cut the lumber so that it isn't touched, which is what they don't do, they don't do it. The only thing they dedicate themselves to is taking the sawn timber, confiscating it. That point I see as bad, right.",
  "CIT137": "The forest was completely intact. There were no people. My father had 120 hectares of land in Volcán. But what they call squatters moved in. And my father had the deed to the farm. And those squatters sold it to a millionaire. A millionaire whose wife was a gringa. And they had money. So, we had no money at all. We did have a few head of cattle, but just working to eat. They did have money. So, they bribed the lawyer that my mother hired. So everything was against us.",
  "CIT138": "I mean, that was poverty, truly, it was poverty, because what you brought lasted eight days and you had to go a month without leaving here because the walk would kill you. You didn't want to go because you'd arrive, I would arrive and not want to; when I arrived at Guerra, the next day I'd wake up and I was exhausted and didn't want to walk because of the walk I'd done. So now it's not like that, now the truth is that there are many people who complain, and even I complain, but you have to look rather at the fact that there are people who are doing well and the government helps them and they're fine, they don't need it.",
  "CIT139": "- Rocío: How do you remember that transition, when was the day you stopped hunting? From hunting to not hunting? - Trino: Well. Here there were many, but almost everyone hunted. Everyone killed the animals. Nobody respected anybody's farm. And nobody said anything, but the moment came when I saw that the animals were coming there to my farm, up here. So I said, we have to protect them because there are few left. The peccaries no longer came here. In all of Rancho Quemado they didn't come because people killed them. And people, the hunter learns that if the animals look for ways to defend themselves so they won't be killed, people hunt all that, right? So people would go to the ridge to wait for them there. So up there they killed them. And so we saw that there were no more peccaries here. The tapirs, there was a time, there were quite a few tapirs here. Even we ourselves at that time chased them with dogs, killed them and all that. So they moved away, the ones that were left moved to the other side of those hills that you can see blue in the distance, there were none here anymore. So when we made the change, well, I began by looking after a herd, and I called it protecting them.",
  "CIT140": "- Trino: No, when we came here, from when we were over there not knowing the place, we already knew it was called Rancho Quemado. So when we came here we began to find out. My father-in-law was a fairly elderly man, say, when this was nothing of mine, we were just getting to know him. He was one of the gold panners who came in there, so they built a little \"ground-rod\" ranch, as we call it, where you cut rods, set two forked posts, a rod across the top on the forks, and start standing rods from the ground up, and then you put leaves on it so the water doesn't come in; that's called a ground-rod ranch. And inside they made a bunk, we call it a platform, again with little forked posts and another rod, and a rack of rods like that, so you spread the meat out here and light a fire there on the ground. So that smoke hits it and that is where the meat dries. At that time what they killed were peccaries, because it's the best meat there is. There are two meats that are the two number ones, which are the wild peccary and the paca, but people always seemed to long more for the peccary. So they killed, and at that time they were fat, they give off lard, so when they went off to get some air, they left the meat smoking, they went to get air, and the meat began to dry and to drip lard and catch fire. When the meat caught fire, the little ranch ignited, and that is why they gave it this name.",
  "CIT141": "- Trino: When they stopped hunting, we have always liked to work, so once we organized them, since we had work, we planted everything, from rice, corn, squash, plantain, cooking banana, and we had pigs, we had hens. We brought two heifers, we brought a horse, and we began to develop them, no more meat from the forest, nothing. So there was rather a surplus here, later on, when we started having animals; we killed, we killed about four sows a year, we killed a cow every year at the end of the year, and chickens. I mean, we gave a lot away to many people.",
  "CIT142": "- Trino: Well, not one specific thing, no, I have several, because everything was new to me. For one, well, I didn't know the sea, nor the Sierpe River, and so many mountains as there were here. But the biggest wish I had, because the first one to come here was a brother of mine named Jeremías Ureña, and when he arrived there he told us, he told us that there was a river here called Río Rillito. So that was my wish, to come to see that river.",
  "CIT143": "- Rocío: What did you do with so much meat? - Trino: We brought it, we skinned the animals, cut it into strips, salted it, and made that smokehouse I'm telling you about, where you suffered the most at the time of drying it. Because once you make that fire, the meat dries, loses its water and then it lasts longer. - Felipe: And where did you keep that meat? - Trino: Well, look, we didn't even have anything to keep it in. What we did was make what we call rod bunks. We cut rods and forked posts and made a sort of platform, a thing, and there we put it. Nothing got to it there.",
  "CIT144": "We arrived at that place. We were bringing a saw. And we were bringing several little things. And they had never heard or seen anything like that. In time, my husband went out and bought a yoke of oxen. I imagine you know them, and a cow and a horse. And when he came in with those animals, the children screamed when they saw them. Because they didn't know anything like that.",
  "CIT145": "I was only used to walking, riding a horse, a motorbike, or a bus. A boat, I didn't even know in a photo. And he says to me: \"in this boat.\" And I say to him: \"I'm not getting in there.\" And he says: \"yes, I have a boat here.\" And I started to cry and cry, because anyway I was already crying, because I was very young, I was barely 19. Because I left my mother, I left my family, I left everything over there, right? And I began to cry, and I tell him: \"I'm not getting in.\" And he says no. And when they put me in the boat and sat me down, I told him, I'll sit down low. And I sat at the back of the boat.",
  "CIT146": "In '75 we started; we arrived there in '74 and in '75 we already had a school in that place. But it was just four posts and sheets of zinc. Four posts, that is, four corner posts and sheets of zinc and nothing more.",
  "CIT147": "We came to build a school; the materials were taken by boat, and with the oxen and the cart they were taken to the place, and we built a school with a cement floor and wooden walls. The lumber was made there in the village, the tree was cut, and we built a school and we built a dining hall.",
  "CIT148": "Sérbulo: Because there were no roads. There were no roads here. They were trails that you had to pass only on foot or on horseback. So we, with shovel, with pick and with a saw, cutting trees, cutting roots and cutting everything, began to fix some roads so that we could pass with a yoke of oxen and a cart. There. Here to Rancho Quemado, toward Drake, toward Sierpe, those roads. So, well, nowadays, thank God, we have access in every direction there is. Right now, at this moment, there are some parts that are still silted up because a lot of the flooding came down these days. Because over from Drake to Sierpe, well, they say you can pass now, but the roads are still quite silted.",
  "CIT149": "Because the neighbors, the whole community that lives here, we are all in agreement to protect, both the animals and nature, and everything there is, because it is very beautiful, it is very beautiful.",
  "CIT150": "And we as campesinos, because I'm going to tell you something, you could offer me right now and tell me: \"we are going to take you to the city.\" A comparison, right, I tell you \"thank you very much, because the city is not for me.\" But if you tell me. You're going to say that we are going to go to that mountain, we're going to go and build a ranch, we're going to go to work, we're going to go walking, we're going to go see animals, live alongside the animals. We'll go, because I'll tell you, because I have loved it.",
  "CIT151": "My husband arrived and began to, he bought that property and began to make that farm. At that time, timber was just thrown into the oven like that. This was the time to say it. What an injustice everything that was done was! Because thanks to God and to the institutions, they have been teaching us how to live.",
  "CIT152": "But this place is a place chosen by God and prospered by minds that are being guided by God. Because that is the secret of this place. Here we began to work in so many ways, and the first way we began to work here is by not felling trees, not killing animals, not destroying what God created, nature.",
  "CIT153": "I was a hunter, but that was thirty years ago. Not just now, that I stopped hunting. It catches my attention: I go to my farm, which is two hundred and thirteen hectares, to clear trails with farmhands. But why do I carry that carbine? Not to kill the animals. Do you know what I kill when it shows up? When I see it, one that doesn't get away on its legs. Because the big teeth appear. Because we kill it then to protect a farmhand, to protect ourselves and all that.",
  "CIT154": "I'm talking about the farm. Because our farm was a place of blessing, everything in life, and it still is. But we're getting on now, my husband can no longer take it on. So we're better off with a caretaker, we pay, there are cows, a few little cows; a daughter has a few little cows together with her husband, so we all pay, we clean, we look after it and everything. But that is what brought us to this place, which is a place of blessing. Why? Because there are many people who love God and also love this place.",
  "CIT155": "Because as we came to find that place, and what it was like now, but just as we came to find it, it later grew; young married couples came out of there, everything came out of there; at that time they went to study, later young people went off to work far away and others went to Drake and set up tourist businesses, and all of that left Estero de Guerra without people. Of all those families that were there, which were something like 5 to 7 families, the ones who had that heap of children, only we remained. The ones who were there, or who have been there now, are people who have moved in. But of all those families, only us.",
  "CIT156": "Well, like... I began by telling you that we lived in a place called Estero de Guerra. The farm is there. We lived 51 years. We arrived at the age of 19. My husband at 19. We lived there 51 years. Lately all our children left home. We were left alone, alone, alone, alone. We lived through many difficulties, illnesses. And many deficiencies in our health. So my husband used to tell me... Someday we'll sell the property to leave here. The years went by. And alone. And then that COVID illness came. And it caught the two of us there all alone. It was very difficult. And I would say to him, \"love, let's get out of here. Let's leave.\" And he would say: \"It's so hard, because how are we going to leave the farm abandoned?\" \"So, my love, it's not necessary to leave it abandoned. We can find someone to look after the property and we go.\" But he would say to me: \"We come from Guanacaste.\" He would say: \"To go there, no.\" I don't want to go to Guanacaste. \"Love, what would happen if we go to Rancho Quemado?\" My son lives here. And many people we know live here.",
  "CIT157": "So we said, we are leaving here, because I must tell you, we lived through very difficult moments, where we cried, we didn't know whether it was water or blood coming out of our eyes, but we never went to bed with an empty stomach. We always had spirit in our eyes, however hard it was. So I want to tell you that the place where we were totally was always a blessing for us, and to this day it is. But for that reason we said, we are leaving here, which is a place of blessing.",
  "CIT158": "My husband arrived and began to, he bought that property and began to make that farm. At that time, timber was just thrown into the oven like that. This was the time to say it. What an injustice everything that was done was! Because thanks to God and to the institutions, they have been teaching us how to live. Because that's how it is. And roads began to be built, and it began. But I remember once my husband was invited to Rancho Quemado. But yes, it did puzzle us: people who were going from Rancho Quemado passed through Estero de Guerra. And we said, but by which way? But why? Why through here? So we began to look. Time went by looking for where those people were passing. And around here, not over here, but over there, on that side, we found a path. But imagine you, in those mountains, a path. Let's say a trail, and that was how the people came down. The only way out the people of Rancho Quemado had. And so the people of Rancho Quemado began to get to know us. They would stay at our houses. And we began to live alongside them and to get to know them.",
  "CIT159": "We had only one daughter, the eldest. My husband came with the boss he had in Arenal de Tilarán. He came to Violín Island. I don't know whether you've heard it mentioned. Yes. Facing the sea, it's in front of the island. They came with the boss to see the farm the boss had there. Where the Salinas are and these other things. I was always from the highlands, from Tilarán upward, and I've never liked being near the sea. The truth of the matter is that he came to see it and liked it, and the boss told him, why don't you come look after this property for me. And he came and told me, let's get ready, we're going. We left the whole family there. We arrived at Sierpe at 5 in the morning in 1973, and he says to me \"here we're going to travel\" and I say \"where to?\" I was only used to walking, riding a horse, a motorbike, or a bus. A boat, I didn't even know in a photo. And he says to me: \"in this boat.\" And I say to him: \"I'm not getting in there.\" And he says: \"yes, I have a boat here.\" And I started to cry and cry, because anyway I was already crying, because I was very young, I was barely 19. Because I left my mother, I left my family, I left everything over there, right? And I began to cry, and I tell him: \"I'm not getting in.\" And he says no. And when they put me in the boat and sat me down, I told him, I'll sit down low. And I sat at the back of the boat. And it was crying and crying, and when they made a certain turn, passing in front of the little town of Sierpe de Osa, I told them: \"please, find a box, find something, please.\" But I need you to cover me so that I don't see that river, so I don't see that expanse where I'm going, because it was wide, wide, it was toward Bosque Sierpe, right? Toward Bosque",
  "CIT160": "So, that has been the situation of these places, and not only Estero Guerra, Sábado, Potreros, all these places are very, very low in everything. So what happens? What there are is farms, farm owners. So the farm owner puts a little farmhand there and that's it. There is no prosperity, right? Let me explain. Why did Rancho Quemado rise later? Because here one or two or three people began to work, and to work and to work. Not with the farm, because please, with the farm, the farm gives very little. Not with the farm, but with the mind, to work in a different way. Having roads, even if they were bad ones, but they began to move and to move, and a beautiful school began and was built, a lovely plaza was made, and it went on and on and on. Where are we? This place is a prosperous place and a great blessing.",
  "CIT161": "What have you planted? - Sérbulo: Well. I have a big list, a really big one. Well, to begin with, fruit: plantain, banana, cooking banana, cassava, chamorro, chiquito, malanga, breadfruit. That we grow. Now, fruit trees: orange, mango, avocado, lemon. All that heap of crops. - Rocío: And do you still have the farm under cultivation now? - Sérbulo: On our farm there is still quite a lot. By the way, I don't know, as I saw a bit, I had a grove of mandarin lemon trees. And by the way, the quantity of lemons there, a lot of lemons came from that. To take and sell to the hotels. The lemon began to take the tree, like a disease, a yellowing disease. And by the way, I heard something recently here. And we here hear that, it's almost like a human being. The news is heard and listened to, that someone comes down to do something about it, but when they get around to doing it, it's already finished. Maybe fifty or more mandarin lemon and lemon trees.",
  "CIT162": "It was an enormous amount of work. It was the same here in Rancho Quemado. Here in Rancho Quemado, I knew Rancho Quemado when we were here, here the pigs, they began a year, two years, three years, looking for forest, cutting it and everything, and planting and everything, and cultivating. You would come and you'd see maybe a couple of dozen, three dozen or more pigs of all sizes. So that was the livelihood for a bunch of families that were here, including this family that lived there. They started out raising pigs in order to survive.",
  "CIT163": "And there we managed to bring a teacher, an educator, and he began to give classes. And the number of children was 45 children. Because yes, there were children then. There were children, I mean, that number was made up of children from 16 years old down to 7 years. Because at that time you only entered school at 7. Not like now, but at 7. It was the number of students that there were in the schools. And we kept working and working and we built a school.",
  "CIT164": "It cost us a lot to convince those children, even with the parents and everything, to go to school. Because they were very nervous. They felt shame, they felt fear and they felt everything. And we come back to the same thing. There were already several, not one, a fairly big group of girls, young women by then and all, but zero schooling. So this teacher says, I'll teach all of them. And we began, she and I, to visit the houses, to go here and there and say to them, look, this is important for you, for you. And so we kept on overwhelming them. And yet they would come to the school, and we would go to the school, and the parents would come, and they were there like shy little ones. And at first, for the first times, for the first months, they hardly did anything.",
  "CIT165": "- Woman 3: Doña Alice, you and the Ureñas, are you part of the founding families of Rancho Quemado? - Alice: Yes, yes, of course. My husband worked a lot to build the school; we came, there were already several kids, so we asked for a teacher. He was the one who ran around doing the errands. They also formed a roads committee, they went to the municipality to ask for help. The municipality helped them with fuel, and I don't know if it was the MINAE, when it was the IDA, I think that's what they called it, yes, and they helped with machinery and the municipality with fuel. So they opened the road, but the people from here didn't even know Venega existed, because everything was toward Guerra, the way out was toward Guerra, and one day my husband set about making a trail with the oldest boy and they came out there at the Venega creek, which now has a bridge; they came out and then they found out there was a little shop.",
  "CIT166": "This school was torn down. Yes, this school was torn down later. But yes, he had built a school, more or less. But when we arrived a little ranch was made with some benches and little tables like that. As he said at that time, some little benches of chonta. Not lumber, it was chonta. And so in that little ranch we had built the teacher taught the children there. During the week. And on Sundays we gathered to pray there, the whole village.",
  "CIT167": "The first year we went in there we decided to plant a lot of rice. And so we planted a lot of rice and harvested a lot. All of us, the kids and I and he, we all planted and harvested. And afterward we saw that those canoes, he made long canoes in the corridor and shared it because there were several kinds. And when we were seeing that those canoes were rather empty, very quick, and there we came over here and left everything alone. We thought nobody stole, and then we realized they were stealing all the rice from us. It was when he had set aside a few little sacks to be seed, and he came and those little sacks were not there.",
  "CIT168": "Yes, yes, they were very fierce. It's said that once you climb up to a little height like this, maybe like this armchair, they won't do anything to you anymore, but if you're down low, yes, because the pigs were going to grab me, and yes, for a long time. One day I was in the part where the hearth is and all that, all of that was closed in with mesh. This girl from conservation, Yolanda, and right now I can't remember what it's called, I mean conservation, that institution, and so they put mesh up at the back, but only at the back, so when the pigs kept coming they entered from here or from this other side, and then they said we want to close it here, and I say to them, and if you close it here and there, and there too, and not on the street side, and if they come in where it comes out, by the street side they didn't leave, I tell you they'll end up worse, shut in here, and I say, no, if you close it, close the whole lot.",
  "CIT169": "I wanted to ask you, one of the things we've also seen is that there are many women who work a lot here, like Jessica, your daughter Alice. How has that situation changed from when you arrived until today, when you see the women and think about yourself back then? - Alice: I worked too much when we arrived here. In fact, here we didn't know masa tamales, because it seems this family was from Chiriquí, the mother, and over there it was only rice tamales. So when we arrived here I told them, but let's make masa tamales, but how do you do that? And I'll tell you. I myself cooked the corn, and early in the morning I was at the school grinding corn. We invited kids, big ones, young boys to come help us, and we made strained masa tamales, and people went crazy because they didn't know those, they didn't know them, and yes, I came to teach them how to make masa tamales, yes. We worked a lot, and my daughter Alice, and now there are Alice, Sandro and Jessica, the three of them are the ones who run everything. Sandro is over there, but from there they take care of everything.",
  "CIT170": "They don't like it here. We had a planting; my husband liked to plant a lot. There were some boys here who were logging, and the boys would say to him, Don Félix, let's eat one, I have a pistol with a silencer, let's eat one, and Félix would say \"no, no, because the MINAE is going to pay me for all this damage,\" I'd better not do anything, I'm not going to eat any peccary or anything. Well, they valued the damages at about 2 million, because they had wrecked everything there, and he didn't eat a single peccary thinking they would pay him; they never gave us a cent. But there are people like that in a line.",
  "CIT171": "So, why didn't the peccaries come back? - Alice: I don't know. It seems those people looked after them so they wouldn't leave. That's what is understood, what has been said, that they were looking after them because they were afraid people would eat them, so they themselves held them back. We never found out. That was what was heard, that they themselves held them back so the peccaries wouldn't leave, but to many neighbors they did far too much damage.",
  "CIT172": "Yes, yes, a little while passed and some families did appear. No, and many families have left, of those who came here to live, they've left. At another time there were more, there were more schoolchildren. I was at the school from 2010 until 2014 and sometimes 79 children came to have lunch, but the teacher had the order to give food to all the little ones who lived around there. So we had to give food to all the little ones, and yes, sometimes up to 79 came. Not now, now there are fewer, I don't know how many there are, but there are fewer.",
  "CIT173": "And why have people left? - Alice: Because of sources of work, there wasn't much work here. And those who had more sources than we did, say, back here all of this that is now pure reserve, that was cattle ranch. So they paid farmhands to clear the pastures and everything, it was a source of work for the people from right here. Also from Joaquín Calderón, from Solís, those farms gave work to people, but then they all decided to sell to MINAE and all for conservation. And that is how the sources of work ended. At least right now people work because they work in Drake. The young men, couples, work in Drake.",
  "CIT174": "The ranch has a sort of framework, but there are crossbeams and there are some up in the roof, so along that framework they ran, they ran along there, and I had a little boy who, well, has died now, he was the third. He had problems like polio, something had struck him when he was small, so he had problems in his right leg and his hand, and they would climb him up to run there. The first one said he couldn't, but they encouraged him and told him he could, and they convinced him, and he says he ran along the crossbeams, and he had trouble walking but, encouraged by the others, he ran along the crossbeams, and he says one day he climbed up and then how was he going to get down, the older ones helped him up but he couldn't get down, so the kids said to him, \"but Cuqui,\" that's what we called him, \"Cuqui, just jump, jump,\" she said to him, Ana Yansi, who is older, who is over at Alice's, \"jump, I'll catch you,\" and he was very stout, very chubby, and she says that when she saw him coming she stepped aside, and he fell onto a chonta bed, and the whole bed fell apart, and thank God nothing happened to him.",
  "CIT175": "And so, well, then my husband says to me, \"well, and afterward, how are you going to get out?\" And I said no, I'm not going to go out, I'm going to have the baby at home. And in fact I stayed in the mountains to have the baby. There was a woman who was a midwife, and so she said, yes, take this and that and that into account, including a bottle of liquor and for treatments, right? For the birth, after the birth. And... And so I stayed at home, and it was hard until 2 in the morning, surely the nerves, well, because I had never given birth to a child at home. So at 2 in the morning the baby was born, and then my husband, from the fright and the emotion that it had been born, surely, grabbed the bottle of corcor liquor, like drinking water, he wouldn't stop. And the woman said to him, \"but leave me the liquor that I need.\" And well, there, with home remedies, I had the baby.",
  "CIT176": "My husband went to the American Embassy. He was president of the education board. He was also working in the association. But at that time he was president of the Education Board, and so he and some others, but the others liked to drink a lot, and they went to the American Embassy to ask for help. It was a plan they called 50-50. And so they gave a little money and the town put up the rest. One sold a hundredweight of beans, another sold corn, another a pig, and so on. And the school was built. It was paid for and the school was built.",
  "CIT177": "Yes. Yes, we lived in Alajuela. In fact, my husband worked there in charge of Bar País in Alajuela. And he also worked in a factory. But we had five children. So the owners of houses no longer wanted to rent us a house because we had many children. The children were going to do some damage, surely. So they didn't want to rent us a house. And my husband worked far too much, at the dance hall and all that. And so the doctor told him to look for the countryside because that was causing him problems. A lot of stress and all that. So we decided; a sister-in-law visited us, a co-sister-in-law, right? She visited us... There in Alajuela, where we lived. And she invited us to come to Drake. Because her husband, who was my husband's brother, had a lot of land. They were vacant lands. So she invited us and my husband was immediately pleased and said yes, he wanted to come see. And he came to see. And I, in truth, I longed to go back to the countryside, because I grew up in the countryside. So he came to see and then, soon after, we came to Drake. From Puntarenas, well, from Alajuela, a car brought us to Ciruelas de Alajuela, where we took the train. That was the train in those days. The train brought us to Puntarenas. In Puntarenas, a launch, 16 hours to Drake.",
  "CIT178": "And my husband worked here with the Ureñas clearing pastures and all that. But after we went to the farm he started to work on his own. Yes, and... In truth, being here, there in Los Ángeles, I had one more child. I went to Heredia with everything I had. The oldest stayed with his father. And I went to pick coffee there. But you know that, pregnant, it was hard for me to pick coffee. The kids were too small to help me pick the coffee. It was a farm that my father rented there, he managed the farm. And there we went to pick coffee. We were there for about... I think I was there about six months. I was already very big-bellied when I decided to come home. And my husband said to me, now what are you going to do? Now what are you going to leave with? Because he had sold a horse he had to give me the money for the expenses to go to the farm. At that time it was cheap, the fare from Los Ángeles to San José, to the airport, was about 90 colones.",
  "CIT179": "Nothing, out front, when there weren't so many plants, you could see all the street here, people lined up their cars to see what animal it was here on this lot, exactly at this cabin down below with the ceramic, there were lots of banana groves, cooking bananas and all that. There the sows made nests and there they gave birth to little red piglets, tiny like that, and so we always saw them, we filmed them and everything, but my concern was, why did the little ones squeal? Because they could all wander around there suddenly for their little ones, and I say, but what could the situation be? And I started to pay attention and it was because the mother went on ahead and left them behind, so the little ones let out a cry, until I discovered that was it.",
  "CIT180": "Sugar, sometimes there was no sugar, but some neighbor had sugarcane and gave us cane, so you cut it into little pieces and crush it and boil it and we drank sweet water, but here it wasn't so much. The worst hardship was there in Los Ángeles because my husband had to travel 2 hours, cross the river and everything, and he would ask the lady of the house to please sell him a little bottle of sour milk, and so when he passed by he'd say \"Doña Su, did you leave me the sour milk?\" \"Ah, I already gave it to the dogs,\" and the sour milk was at least to help get the plantains down, because what we did was cooked plantains and coffee without sugar or anything, yes. We had a hard time there too; we cooked green beans from plants my husband had sown, and we kept giving the kids green beans, chopped green beans, and the kids all broke out in their skin, because they say that the green bean, I don't know if it was very heating, but the thing is it did them harm, because you can eat chopped green beans but with rice, with other things, but it was only that, so that affected the health of the two oldest a lot when we were there in Los Ángeles. Here it was a little different. Afterwards I started raising hens, hatching chicks, but I went away for a while, I went to Heredia because I was going to have a baby, and the hens were already grown and there was a big crib that we had filled with clothes, there was nowhere to put the clothes.",
  "CIT181": "They came from Volcán de Buenos Aires. I don't know how they got in; I understand it was through Sierpe, along the Sierpe River up to Guerra through the estuaries. And they arrived here. I think the first to come was Jeremías, of those who came from there, but he wasn't actually the first to enter here; there were other men from around here who had already come hunting and had burned a ranch. They would leave the fire lit, the meat smoking, and head back into the forest. And then the fat from the pigs caught fire in the ranch and it burned up by the time they came back, no clothes or anything, because everything of theirs burned. And that's where Rancho Quemado comes from.",
  "CIT182": "I worked too much when we arrived here. In fact, here we didn't know masa tamales, because it seems this family was from Chiriquí, the mother, and over there it was only rice tamales. So when we arrived here I told them, but let's make masa tamales, but how do you do that? And I'll tell you. I myself cooked the corn, and early in the morning I was at the school grinding corn. We invited kids, big ones, young boys to come help us, and we made strained masa tamales, and people went crazy because they didn't know those, they didn't know them, and yes, I came to teach them how to make masa tamales, yes. We worked a lot, and my daughter Alice, and now there are Alice, Sandro and Jessica, the three of us are the ones who run everything. Sandro is over there, but from there they take care of everything.",
  "CIT183": "The river, that was another thing; my husband was very uncomfortable on that farm, a very pretty farm but it had no water. He managed to dig a well, he made it very deep, and the water seeped in and was drawn out, in the summer. In the winter there was a little creek, and with that watercourse things were done and washed, but for washing clothes you had to go to the river. I went to the river pregnant with the oldest girl, and afterward we would lay the clothes on the stone to drain them a little so as not to bring them home so wet. Then throw a sack of clothes over our shoulder to come home with a sack of clothes. I even put a sack of clothes on myself while I was pregnant, and that child was born sick because I did far too many chores, and once there was a hurricane in Alajuela, no, an earthquake, they said the graves opened, you may remember that.",
  "CIT184": "No, they were playing around there. Well, I have one who now lives in Puerto Jiménez, he used to play with the neighbor who is Enrique's cousin, so they played that they were horses; sometimes one was the horse, sometimes the other, and the one who was the horse, the other pulled him with a rope and loaded him up with a green bunch of plantains, and the other ate the plantain because he was the horse. They invented many more things that they did; they invented, or climbing trees, that was tremendous, it was a whole odyssey those kids lived through, but thank God they all finished sixth grade here. My husband wanted to send them to study, but they showed up with bad company there, and then he realized that the paths were going badly because they studied here, more or less by adult-education, but went to take the presentations in Villa Neily and were doing very badly, so he no longer wanted to send them. Those who want to study study on their own, because I can't, with him making sacrifices and us doing that.",
  "CIT185": "How about the smell of the peccaries when they have sighted them? They always give off an alert that there is danger, and they release that smell. How do you experience it? Pleasant, unpleasant? - Yared: To me it smells good already. - Alberto: It already smells good to you? - Yared: I like it, but it always smells like a stinky armpit. - Alberto: No, but they have their own smell. - Yared: Yes, but it's similar to an armpit.",
  "CIT186": "\"Look at the size of the wild peccary's track. A normal peccary has a track half this size. When a peccary shows this track, this size of hoof on the ground, it's because it's an adult peccary.\"",
  "CIT187": "Mmmm, I mean, from when my mom started, I was little and I only went to school. So I don't remember very well, and later, when I grew up a little, I was already being dragged along through the mountains. I'd climb up anywhere when I heard something, because she would say the peccaries were coming. One day my sister and I went for a walk, and there was a huge, tall trunk, and we began to hear something, and a smell was coming, but the collared peccaries had already passed and left the smell lying there. So Mom says, \"run, and come back and climb that tree,\" and Mom went off again into the forest, and when she got there it was monkeys, which she thought were the peccaries, and I was already running all scared, asking what was wrong with Mom, and the two of us climbed up while she was off in the forest.",
  "CIT188": "Of everything, of everything of everything, yes, because there was a biologist here too who studied, I don't know whether some of you had a chance to meet him, a Brazilian, Robert Val. When he was here doing his thesis and left, he declared the curassow extinct for Rancho Quemado. Then, years later, while we have been working, he came, and there is one person who does treat it the way he says, and he asks us, \"how is the curassow population? Because curassows are an indicator in the system,\" and we tell him, \"no, they're here, there are loads of them.\" He says to me, \"that can't be possible, because for me it was extinct in Rancho Quemado at the time I left.\" And well, it wasn't far off, but thanks to the care and everything that has been done, the population has increased. \"And do you have a way to back up what you're saying?\" Yes, I mean, precisely because we have camera traps and we have seen 7 females pass together in front of the camera, and that is a piece of data that gives us a great deal of information, and we know that yes, all the populations have increased, and in different sites, where for two years I went with Yolanda to monitor and found only one lizard.",
  "CIT189": "80, and that herd was eliminated by people, and they came from several places, they came here to kill them, and as a spectacle they were there at the edge of the road and crossed the street and were at the edge of the school. While they were at the edge of the school, nobody killed them, because if the MINAE people came there, but they moved from one side to another, and when they moved they killed them. And so, as that herd was eliminated, you see that the peccaries really were going to go extinct. So, say, from there on is when this began.",
  "CIT190": "This plant, the coligallo, suita, was heavily persecuted in the time when resources were many, few, scarce. Here the campesino chose to cut it and build his ranch. This is the suita for making ranches. And one of the factors was that at that time people used ranches a lot. So by cutting a lot of suita, the wild peccary also had to pull from here because there was no food for them.",
  "CIT191": "In a short time, how that response has been, the results of putting in a little bit of love, because as I was telling some colleagues last weekend, we were giving a talk to a brigade that wants to be set up on the coastal ridge. And so we told them, if you are going to do things, do them out of love; if you are going to do it thinking you'll get money from it, better not do it, because it won't work. It doesn't work that way; it's out of love, it's out of empowerment for what we have, because this belongs to all of us, and the only ones responsible for making sure everything is well are us, that everything keeps going well. If we don't value what we have, I mean, I don't know how much a tourist from another country pays to come see what we have here, and we have it and don't value it. But it's a matter of taking a little time, because then you come and start to fall in love and you say, \"what day are we going walking?\"",
  "CIT192": "So the hunter could enter because a farmer called him, the protector who was perhaps a small grain of the protection of that time, which there wasn't really; so there was more and more hunting in Rancho Quemado. And that was my beginning; I learned to hunt to survive, not to sell, to feed the family. And as time went by we managed to enter the heart of Rancho, and we came down because we lived a bit farther from the center. And I started to feel that hunting was not good, that hunting was not good, and when I married Nuria, she made me understand that we were doing the wrong thing. And I began to go into mental training, which helped me to change from being a hunter to becoming a protector, which today has served us a lot, and my companions who today have a lot of knowledge of the forest have it because they were hunters.",
  "CIT193": "To feel happy, because the truth is that a lot was achieved, a lot was achieved, and thank God I think this continues. And the truth is that alone one had nothing; thanks be to God for the group we have, and for the people who cooperated so much, in the sense that even if they didn't go to the meetings or into the forest with us, they supported us as a group. Many stopped hunting, and some who kept hunting gradually withdrew from hunting in order to support us, but it was achieved and it has achieved a lot. At least I was watching a video that maybe you've seen, of a man who throws cassava to a bunch of curassows and they come out and come out, have you not seen it, look what a lovely thing, but thank God, no, what's it called. Well, we achieved it too, only we are not going to stop feeding them, right, because they don't need it in the first place, and that can't be done. One day I came and on that other side, there by the palm, there were nine of those big curassows, nine females and six males, all in a single group. They come out of the forest to eat breadfruit. At the beginning, when I planted the palm, I thought about it, because some government institution said it wasn't good to plant palm here because the monkeys would fill up with cholesterol, and several animals. So I thought about it, but I saw them, and the truth is that they don't know how to eat palm like that, maybe they don't eat it, listen, and all kinds of animals come in here, and well, we haven't run tests on them, but I think that's pure lie that it gives them cholesterol, because the animals are normal.",
  "CIT194": "Nuria: Yes, since 2019 we have seen the increase in the herds quite notably, which is the first thing they sometimes ask us, whether in the work we've done we have seen a change in nature's response, so to speak, and, I mean, the response has been super positive. Well, in fact you have seen in the little we've walked and what has been observed, and likewise the population, the density of the population has increased a lot, in all species. Because sometimes they ask us that, but \"do you only look after the peccaries?\" Well, no. I mean, we began focused on that, on the peccaries, but while looking after the peccaries, for example I see a bird and that's my distraction, I get lost watching what it's doing and everything. And so if we sit down to wait for the peccaries to be sleeping or something, you see that the monkeys are there, that the monkeys are eating and below the peccaries are eating, that there are birds eating there, with the peccaries below. So all of that has been a chain of learning and it is something that never ends, because every time one comes or passes through the same place, one always sees something different, always, and learns about everything. Sometimes they ask you, what's the name of that plant, but you have a little bit of everything, and sometimes you get a knot in your brain and don't know whether it's a bird, a plant or a mammal, but you do know that it exists.",
  "CIT195": "And that was my beginning; I learned to hunt to survive, not to sell, to feed the family. And as time went by we managed to enter the heart of Rancho, and we came down because we lived a bit farther from the center. And I started to feel that hunting was not good, that hunting was not good, and when I married Nuria, she made me understand that we were doing the wrong thing. And I began to go into mental training, which helped me to change from being a hunter to becoming a protector, which today has served us a lot, and my companions who today have a lot of knowledge of the forest have it because they were hunters. And that knowledge has been very lethal for protection in Rancho Quemado.",
  "CIT196": "And perhaps it hurt the ego a bit, because at that time the Extra newspaper put out, I think it was the Extra, a really big headline that said \"herd of wild peccaries comes down to the community of Rancho Quemado and is exterminated\" (28:05). And we, I mean, when people picked up the newspaper, which was very common at that time, picking up the newspaper, and everybody stood looking at it, and \"did you see what came out in the paper,\" \"did you see what came out on the front page of the paper,\" and it was like a pile of peccary heads on top of a pole, and everybody was stunned. I mean, yes, some peccaries were killed by people from Rancho, but it wasn't the people of Rancho who exterminated them; it was people from Pérez Zeledón, from San Vito, from everywhere who killed them and exterminated them, and seeing the headline that said Rancho Quemado. I feel that there was also that spark there, like, either we shake ourselves or we are going to finish sinking, because in any case Rancho already had a reputation, right?",
  "CIT197": "From 2019 on, the forest centers. There has been a change in the work until reaching looking after 4 different herds, and the smallest herd there was had 40, and say that from one to another it was 3 kilometers, 4 kilometers, 5 kilometers. So they weren't the same, and there were already 4 groups looking after different herds. - Nuria: Yes, since 2019 we have seen the increase in the herds quite notably, which is the first thing they sometimes ask us, whether in the work we've done we have seen a change in nature's response, so to speak, and, I mean, the response has been super positive. Well, in fact you have seen in the little we've walked and what has been observed, and likewise the population, the density of the population has increased a lot, in all species. Because sometimes they ask us that, but \"do you only look after the peccaries?\" Well, no. I mean, we began focused on that, on the peccaries, but while looking after the peccaries, for example I see a bird and that's my distraction, I get lost watching what it's doing and everything.",
  "CIT198": "If we don't value what we have, I mean, I don't know how much a tourist from another country pays to come see what we have here, and we have it and don't value it. But it's a matter of taking a little time, because then you come and start to fall in love and you say, \"what day are we going walking?\" We're going to go see new flowers, we're going to go see new seedlings, we're going to go see animals. We still have a whole world left to learn, but every day one falls more in love, and the beautiful part is also transmitting that, because many times they tell us, you know, when you talk you can feel what you are talking about, you are transmitting it.",
  "CIT199": "Of everything, of everything of everything, yes, because there was a biologist here too who studied, I don't know whether some of you had a chance to meet him, a Brazilian, Robert Val. When he was here doing his thesis and left, he declared the curassow extinct for Rancho Quemado. Then, years later, while we have been working, he came, and there is one person who does treat it the way he says, and he asks us, \"how is the curassow population? Because curassows are an indicator in the system,\" and we tell him, \"no, they're here, there are loads of them.\" He says to me, \"that can't be possible, because for me it was extinct in Rancho Quemado at the time I left.\" And well, it wasn't far off, but thanks to the care and everything that has been done, the population has increased. \"And do you have a way to back up what you're saying?\" Yes, I mean, precisely because we have camera traps and we have seen 7 females pass together in front of the camera, and that is a piece of data that gives us a great deal of information, and we know that yes, all the populations have increased, and in different sites, where for two years I went with Yolanda to monitor and found only one lizard.",
  "CIT200": "The wildlife shows it to you in reality, the animals; and precisely for there to be a good team, it's not just one person who is good, but the whole group, and in fact the group that was formed is what makes it solid, so that the moment an animal dies in another town, here it hurts us. Because it has cost us a great deal over these years and we have done it out of love, as Nuria said on occasion, and so it has been. The reality, the reality of seeing that you begin to give a grain of possibility to nature and another person destroys it, it makes you furious.",
  "CIT201": "Going back to the peccaries, last night we were remembering, right, that perhaps unconsciously, also in 2008, a herd came down that, by the way, was completely wiped out. They crossed into the community and passed a lot behind the school because there were some peach palm and vallisneria plants, and at that time people said, but those peccaries won't leave there, the MINAE doesn't get them out of there, because that is dangerous and I don't know what.",
  "CIT202": "And it is like the idea that everyone brings about that change and we try to recover what we have already lost, because we have had a lot and we have lost a lot, and we don't know the consequences either. Sometimes the consequences of many illnesses and those things come from things we ignore, and that we ourselves are the cause of, and sometimes the easy thing is to blame the bat or whatever, and in the end the culprits are ourselves.",
  "CIT203": "Yes, but when you have to come into the forest you have to come without perfume, so they don't smell the cologne. - Alberto: What did you just say, Yared? Why do you have to come without scents or colognes? - Yared: So the critters don't leave, so they don't smell it and go off. - Alberto: One of the things that, when you come as a guide into the forest, you tell your companion is that before coming, biologists and such are used to putting on colognes or very strong repellents. Don't use very strong scents, because animals have a more sensitive and stronger sense of smell than any human being. We are used to rubbing something on, so the custom is a lotion or a repellent to go into the forest for protection from mosquito bites. And what Yared was referring to was that, that if we want to see the peccaries or the tapirs or the animals as little disturbed as possible, ideally we go smelly. And that is how we have managed it, because when we have gone with new groups and they bring colognes, what they do is that, at the moment when we are about to have an encounter with herds, they sniff right away what is around. So what they sense is a non-communicative smell from them, which is not the forest, so they take it as danger, a predator. So what they do is run away.",
  "CIT204": "There are the ant-tanagers, which are... Well, the distribution has shifted a bit further. But it used to be in Véniza of the Peninsula, and the distribution keeps moving a bit further. There's the gray-headed tanager. And the other ant-followers. I suppose the drovers are around there. Because look how they look on the ground. - Kai: That's the one that laughs. - Nuria: We spent a year behind them, behind the ant-tanagers, to see the behavior, the distribution, the reproduction, everything. - Kai: Was it harder? - Nuria: Yes, they are very... No, his father no. They are very... Well, right now it's because the ant-followers are fixed there. But they are very, very active when they have a nest. And since we knew practically nothing, it was like following behind, behind, behind, all day. Until we saw where they nested. Then we began to see what plants they liked for nesting. So we were going around checking all the little shrubs for nests. Then we also set up camera traps, because we would see the nests and quickly the chicks weren't there, because they throw themselves to the ground without feathers.",
  "CIT205": "Mmmm, I mean, from when my mom started, I was little and I only went to school. So I don't remember very well, and later, when I grew up a little, I was already being dragged along through the mountains. I'd climb up anywhere when I heard something, because she would say the peccaries were coming. One day my sister and I went for a walk, and there was a huge, tall trunk, and we began to hear something, and a smell was coming, but the collared peccaries had already passed and left the smell lying there. So Mom says, \"run, and come back and climb that tree,\" and Mom went off again into the forest, and when she got there it was monkeys, which she thought were the peccaries, and I was already running all scared, asking what was wrong with Mom, and the two of us climbed up while she was off in the forest.",
  "CIT206": "- Alberto: If there's something that makes the difference in a team today, it's the beginning, and the beginning was a person who started; for there to be a start, there has to be a beginning, and the one who began, I think, here, to look after and to give a spark, as Nuria said, of change. So to speak, to me it seems it was Don Trino Ureña, because he raised the family in the difficult time, and as time went by I heard them say, \"let's plant the palm; if it's no good for the economy, it's good for feeding the animals.\" So he wanted to make a change since then, and we are talking about how many years ago this palm was planted, Trino? - Trino: 14 years.",
  "CIT207": "My trauma, my trauma. Well, I still, the peccaries, if I haven't lost the fear of them, I'm still afraid of them, but my father hunted peccaries and, I mean, they went to hunt them super far away, because maybe they saw them here, but peccaries walk very fast and if they're always being chased, even more. So they killed them far away and got to the house at 5 or 6 in the afternoon with the peccaries, and they arrived with everything. I mean, the only thing they didn't bring was the peccary's guts, but they came with the hide, with the head, and they arrived with those peccary heads, in half a drum, he cut a drum in half, and in half a drum they put those heads to cook to feed to the dogs, and so, since it was already dark, I was sent to stoke the fire so it would cook. So I'd arrive with a kerosene lamp, which is what we had in those days, and light up the drum and see the tusks and some blue eyes inside that drum, and when I went backward my lamp would go out from the speed I took. I'd stoke that fire and run away, I felt those creatures behind me, and I grew up with that fear and still, I mean, today I still see them, and I know that keeping my distance and everything they won't come after you, because I've seen their behavior a lot during all these years; it's something that stays with you, right, a childhood trauma. But the beautiful thing is that, I mean, that whole situation has been reversed here, and the hope is that it can be seen in other communities.",
  "CIT208": "And the smell, how about it, Yared? - Yared: Ew! - Nuria: Look over there. - Alberto: How about the smell of the peccaries when they have sighted them? They always give off an alert that there is danger, and they release that smell. How do you experience it? Pleasant, unpleasant? - Yared: To me it smells good already. - Alberto: It already smells good to you? - Yared: I like it, but it always smells like a stinky armpit. - Alberto: No, but they have their own smell. - Yared: Yes, but it's similar to an armpit.",
  "CIT209": "Here the herds are, well, the first ones that began to arrive were 40 and up, and now they always run within a range of herds, because they keep exchanging among themselves, between 60 and 100 and 50 and 80 like that. They keep changing, they split up or make changes within the herd, that is, males and females, for the protection of their own genes.",
  "CIT210": "\"Well, it's very easy. If you serve as a guide, they follow you and they keep learning and you keep explaining to them, and there they are seeing it, they commit.\""
}

